me\": \"2-hydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactonitrile; unresolved stereoisomers\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H5NO/c1-3(5)2-4/h3,5H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alaninonitrile; unresolved stereoisomers\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-methylimidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"5-methylhydantoin; unresolved stereoisomers\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Awaruite-mediated hydrogen-dependent CO2 fixation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_002\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"awaruite, Ni3Fe\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373 K; consistent with the temperature reported in the supplied review\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed combined H2 and CO2 partial pressure of 25 bar, with H2:CO2 = 4:1; water vapor contributes additional pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"awaruite, Ni3Fe\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: pH 7.5-9.5, 10-100 g/L characterized mineral, and 16-48 h residence. Maintain sufficient alkalinity under CO2 loading. Quantify mineral corrosion, carbon-containing blanks, and pyruvate formation using labeled CO2. After formation, cool and transfer locally to the amination zone rather than assuming ocean-scale concentration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Empirical net carbon fixation: 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvic acid + 3 H2O. Alloy surfaces activate hydrogen and promote carbon reduction and coupling. A unique sequence of soluble C1 intermediates is not asserted.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) support hydrogen-dependent mineral-mediated formation of metabolic intermediates. Awaruite is chemically justified by its occurrence in serpentinized rocks and its hydrogen-activation capability. This branch evaluates an H2-fed alloy system without silently treating added Fe0 as a renewable catalyst. The supplied summary does not establish its exact mineral-specific yield under the proposed recipe; pyruvate identity, carbon provenance, catalyst stability, and productive flux require confirmation. Pyruvate decomposition and dilution remain competing processes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Magnetite-mediated hydrogen-dependent CO2 fixation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_002\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"magnetite, Fe3O4\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373 K; consistent with the temperature reported in the supplied review\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed combined H2 and CO2 partial pressure of 25 bar, with H2:CO2 = 4:1; water vapor contributes additional pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite, Fe3O4\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: pH 7.5-9.5, 10-100 g/L characterized magnetite, and 16-48 h residence. Exclude O2 and document mineral phases before and after reaction. Distinguish pyruvate from other reduced-carbon products and organic contamination. Rapid local cooling and retention are required before amination.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same balanced net conversion as rxn_001 is evaluated on an iron-oxide surface. Surface adsorption and redox chemistry can facilitate CO2 reduction; the detailed carbon-coupling mechanism is unresolved.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied account of Preiner et al. (2020) identifies magnetite as an alternative mineral system. It does not justify assuming the same pyruvate yield or selectivity as awaruite, so this is a separate mineral-specific evaluation branch rather than an independent confirmation of the entire alanine pathway. Magnetite's structural Fe(III) does not require molecular oxygen in the reaction. H2 must supply the reducing equivalents assumed by the net equation; substantial mineral consumption would require revised material accounting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Ferrous-ion-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"dissolved iron(II) ions\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K, the reported laboratory temperature; interpreted as a cool hydrothermal mixing-zone analogue\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed 10-50 bar for a shallow subsurface implementation; pressure is not established as a requirement by the supplied report\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"iron(II) ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed evaluation window: pH 4.5-6.5, 1-10 mM oxaloacetate, 1-10 mM dissolved Fe2+, and 0.5-24 h residence. Avoid sulfide-rich or phosphate-rich carryover that removes dissolved iron. Capture pyruvate before extended heating. External oxaloacetate availability is an explicit feedstock assumption.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lewis-acid coordination facilitates beta-keto-acid decarboxylation. The resulting enol tautomerizes to pyruvate: oxaloacetic acid -> pyruvic acid + CO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019) provide the strongest supplied support for this upstream conversion, including operation at 343 K with iron species. Fe2+ is justified as a carbonyl-coordinating Lewis acid, not as a stoichiometric oxidant. The proposed concentrations and residence window are not claimed as the paper's exact protocol. Oxaloacetate instability supports its conversion but also makes its accumulation and delivery a separate limitation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Unassisted surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed evaluation window: pH 4-7, 1-10 mM oxaloacetate, and 1-7 days. Use shaded aqueous pores because UV is unnecessary and can destroy keto acids. For subsequent rxn_005, require short-distance anoxic infiltration into a cooled Fe0-bearing water-rock zone, with measured dilution and adjustment to the amination pH window.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thermal beta-keto-acid decarboxylation followed by enol-keto tautomerization gives pyruvate and CO2 without an external redox reagent.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson (2021) is cited in the supplied review for oxaloacetate-to-pyruvate decarboxylation in surface chemistry. The uncatalyzed window here is a proposed implementation based on established beta-keto-acid reactivity, not a claim to reproduce the reported one-hour experiment. Sulfur and UV are not assigned catalytic roles without evidence. This branch differs from rxn_003 in catalyst requirement and setting, but represents the same underlying carbon transformation. Delivery to the iron-driven amination zone is conditional, not demonstrated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Sacrificial-iron reduction of the pyruvate-hydroxylamine condensation product\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"iron(II) ions as Lewis-acid promoter; elemental iron is an explicitly consumed reductant\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K, as reported for the iron-promoted amino-acid chemistry\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed 10-50 bar in a cooled subsurface water-rock zone; elevated pressure is not asserted to be necessary for the laboratory reaction\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"iron(II) ions; Fe0 is a sacrificial reagent rather than a catalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed evaluation window: pH 4.5-6.5, 1-10 mM pyruvate, 1-3 equivalents hydroxylamine, and 1-48 h residence. Supply at least the net requirement of two Fe atoms per alanine molecule, with additional iron potentially lost to corrosion. Maintain a proton reservoir, measure dissolved Fe2+, and exclude sulfide or carbonate carryover that passivates the metal. Hydroxylamine supply and competing decomposition must be measured.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvate condenses with hydroxylamine to an oxime-like intermediate, followed by four-electron reduction involving C=N reduction and N-O cleavage. Per alanine molecule, the balanced reference equation is pyruvic acid + NH2OH + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> alanine + 2 Fe2+ + 2 H2O. Achiral conditions produce both alanine enantiomers.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019) support iron-promoted conversion of keto acids to amino acids using hydroxylamine. The two water-placement variants in the supplied review are not counted as separate mechanisms: without a reductant they are incomplete redox equations. Here Fe0 consumption, proton demand, and monatomic Fe2+ production are explicit. Hydroxylamine is not treated as an automatically abundant geochemical feedstock. Neither this reaction nor the supplied evidence establishes an L-enantiomeric excess.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde Strecker synthesis through imine capture\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar, with restricted gas exchange\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed evaluation window: pH 8-9.5, 1-20 mM acetaldehyde, 1-50 mM total cyanide, 0.1-1 M total ammonia, and 1-7 days. Gas-retaining, optically shielded pores are required to limit acetaldehyde, HCN, and NH3 loss. These concentrated inventories require an independently justified accumulation mechanism. Subsequent acidic hydrolysis must occur in a separate pH stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetaldehyde and ammonia reversibly form an imine or iminium species. Cyanide addition gives unresolved 2-aminopropanenitrile. The net conversion releases water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Classical alanine Strecker chemistry provides direct chemical precedent (Strecker, 1850), while Patel et al. (2015) support aminonitrile-based amino-acid precursor chemistry in a prebiotic framework. Acetaldehyde is explicitly supplied rather than assumed to be selectively recovered from pyruvate degradation. The main environmental limitations are volatile-feedstock retention, ammonia concentration, competing cyanohydrin formation, and aminonitrile survival under surface UV. No mineral is assigned an unsupported catalytic function.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible capture of acetaldehyde as lactonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar, with restricted gas exchange\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed evaluation window: pH 8-9, 1-20 mM acetaldehyde, 1-50 mM total cyanide, and hours to days. Retain the aqueous phase in shaded pores. Cyanide supplied by HCN dissociation is the nucleophile; no additional mineral catalyst is assumed. Measure equilibrium partitioning rather than assuming quantitative aldehyde storage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, and proton transfer gives the cyanohydrin lactonitrile. Formation is reversible and produces an unresolved stereoisomer population.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is established cyanohydrin chemistry, but the supplied report does not directly validate this particular feed-storage implementation. It is therefore speculative as an integrated prebiotic branch; Patel et al. (2015) provide contextual support for cyanide-derived precursor chemistry, not a measured yield for this proposed step. Inclusion is justified because reversible cyanohydrin formation can change the retention and timing of a volatile aldehyde feed, rather than because it creates a nominal extra pathway.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven equilibration of lactonitrile into alaninonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar, with restricted gas exchange\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed evaluation window: pH 8-9.5, 1-20 mM lactonitrile, 0.1-1 M total ammonia, and 1-7 days. Retain released HCN and acetaldehyde and shield the solution from destructive UV. Determine the lactonitrile/aminonitrile distribution experimentally; ammonia addition alone does not establish a useful conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lactonitrile reversibly releases acetaldehyde and HCN. Ammonia captures acetaldehyde through imine formation, after which cyanide addition gives alaninonitrile. The net equation is lactonitrile + NH3 -> alaninonitrile + H2O; direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group is not assumed.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The chemical basis is the coupled cyanohydrin and Strecker equilibria, with classical Strecker precedent (Strecker, 1850). The supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary does not establish this exact feeding sequence, so its prebiotic implementation is speculative. This is an alternative entry through a retained precursor pool, not a new fundamental C-N bond-forming mechanism or an independent demonstration of alanine synthesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-promoted alaninonitrile hydrolysis to racemic alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"hydronium ions supplied by acidic volcanic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed environmental test window: pH 1-2, 1-20 mM alaninonitrile, and 1-30 days in shaded hot-spring water. This milder acid regime is not asserted to match a concentrated-acid analytical hydrolysis protocol. Stage acidification after aminonitrile formation; quantify both alaninamide accumulation and alanine release. At reaction pH, ammonia and alanine are protonated; the graph uses neutral reference species.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid activation of the nitrile permits water addition, formation of alaninamide, and subsequent amide hydrolysis. Per aminonitrile molecule, two water molecules yield alanine and ammonia. Both hydrolytic transformations are included in this lumped reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support alaninonitrile as an alanine precursor. The review's ambient-aqueous description does not establish rapid, complete hydrolysis at neutral pH. Heating, acid availability, and the slower amide-cleavage stage are therefore explicit constraints. Acidic volcanic waters offer a chemically plausible catalyst source, but useful conversion in this proposed environmental window must be demonstrated. No chiral selector is present, so L- and D-alanine are both products.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline alaninonitrile hydrolysis to racemic alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"hydroxide supplied by concentrated alkaline water-rock fluids\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydroxide ion as base promoter\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed evaluation window: cooled-sample pH 11.5-12.5, 1-20 mM alaninonitrile, and 1-30 days. Requires unusually alkaline, retained geothermal water rather than ordinary seawater. Monitor alkalinity consumption, alaninamide accumulation, ammonia escape, and competing aminonitrile degradation. Alaninate is the environmental product; subsequent acid-base equilibration is implicit in the neutral reference representation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide addition to the nitrile leads to an amide intermediate; further base-promoted acyl substitution produces alaninate and ammonia. Neutral-reference atom balance is alaninonitrile + 2 H2O -> alanine + NH3.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support the precursor-to-amino-acid relationship, but the supplied review does not establish this alkaline recipe or its yield. This is a condition-dependent implementation of ordinary nitrile and amide hydrolysis, not an independent carbon-skeleton synthesis. Its chemical distinction from rxn_009 is base-promoted rather than acid-promoted hydrolysis. Strong alkalinity must be geochemically available, and failure to hydrolyze the amide efficiently would block the pathway.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbon dioxide capture of alaninonitrile as 5-methylhydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in a CO2-retaining aqueous pocket\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed evaluation window: pH 8-9.5, 1-20 mM alaninonitrile, 0.05-0.5 M dissolved inorganic carbon, and 1-7 days. An ammonia/carbonate reservoir buffers the solution without supplying additional net nitrogen to this step. The concentration and carbon-retention requirements are substantial. No organic cosolvent is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminonitrile carbamylation by CO2, intramolecular cyclization, and rearrangement generate 5-methylhydantoin through Bucherer-Bergs-type chemistry. The balanced net equation is alaninonitrile + CO2 -> 5-methylhydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Classical hydantoin synthesis provides additional chemical precedent (Bucherer and Lieb, 1934), but this pathway is not supported by the supplied prebiotic studies and is explicitly speculative as a geochemical implementation. It is included because trapping a labile aminonitrile in a cyclic ureide could alter precursor persistence and release, not because hydantoin formation establishes alanine availability. Formation in water alone, competition with simple hydrolysis, and the required carbonate concentration must be tested.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal alkaline hydrolysis of 5-methylhydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_002\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"hydroxide supplied by hyperalkaline water-rock fluids\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"423-453 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed 30-100 bar to retain liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydroxide ion as base promoter\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Speculative evaluation window: cooled-sample pH 12-13, 1-20 mM hydantoin, and 1-7 days. Require short-distance infiltration from a surface geothermal pocket into a retained alkaline subsurface fluid, not assumed transport from oceanic vents to surface pools. Measure N-carbamoylalanine accumulation, total alanine yield, and thermal destruction. These conditions are demanding and may not provide useful selectivity.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic ring opening forms N-carbamoylalanine; subsequent carbamoyl cleavage releases alanine, ammonia, and inorganic carbon. Per hydantoin molecule, the neutral-reference balance is 5-methylhydantoin + 2 H2O -> alanine + NH3 + CO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin-to-amino-acid hydrolysis is established preparative chemistry associated with hydantoin synthesis, but the supplied research provides no validation of this environmental implementation. This reaction is explicitly speculative and is the principal bottleneck of the hydantoin branches. Classical chemical precedent, including Bucherer and Lieb (1934), does not establish selective hydrolysis in natural hyperalkaline fluids. Alanine destruction, incomplete carbamoyl cleavage, alkalinity availability, and transport could each prevent productive flux.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Awaruite CO2 fixation followed by iron-driven amination\",\n      \"description\": \"CO2 and H2 supply pyruvate on an alloy surface; hydroxylamine and sacrificial Fe0 then furnish racemic alanine containing the L target. Mineral-specific pyruvate production, local retention, and hydroxylamine supply are required. The two stages need different pH conditions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Magnetite CO2 fixation followed by iron-driven amination\",\n      \"description\": \"An oxide-mineral alternative supplies the same pyruvate intermediate for the common amination step. This is a catalyst-distinct graph route, not evidence that magnetite and awaruite have equal yields or that the complete sequence has been demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Ferrous oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied oxaloacetate undergoes Fe2+-promoted decarboxylation before the supported hydroxylamine/Fe0 amination chemistry. This is among the strongest supplied reaction combinations, but oxaloacetate and hydroxylamine availability remain outside the demonstrated network boundary.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface oxaloacetate decay followed by subsurface amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Unassisted surface decarboxylation supplies pyruvate, which must enter a nearby cooled Fe0-bearing subsurface zone without excessive dilution or degradation. The transfer, pH staging, and metal-passivation constraints are explicit prerequisites rather than demonstrated processes.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker entry with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde, ammonia, and HCN form alaninonitrile through imine capture, followed by a separate acidic hot-spring hydrolysis stage. Both nitrile hydration and amide cleavage must proceed; the product is racemic alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker entry with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same Strecker precursor is processed in an alkaline geothermal water rather than an acidic pool. This is a hydrolysis-condition alternative to P5, not an independent amino-acid carbon-skeleton synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker entry with hydantoin storage and release\",\n      \"description\": \"Alaninonitrile is conditionally captured with CO2 as 5-methylhydantoin before hydrothermal hydrolysis. The hydantoin formation and release implementation is speculative; its value would depend on improved precursor persistence outweighing the demanding release conditions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-fed Strecker entry with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde first enters a reversible lactonitrile pool. Ammonia-driven equilibration supplies alaninonitrile for acidic hydrolysis. The cyanohydrin-feeding implementation is conditional and shares the fundamental Strecker bond-forming chemistry with P5.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-fed Strecker entry with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A retained lactonitrile pool feeds alaninonitrile formation, followed by alkaline hydrolysis. Productive flux requires favorable coupled equilibria, volatile retention, and sufficient alkalinity for both hydrolytic stages.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-fed hydantoin storage-and-release pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Lactonitrile feeds alaninonitrile, which is captured as a hydantoin and subsequently hydrolyzed. This is the most conditional route: neither the combined feed-storage sequence nor selective environmental hydantoin release is established by the supplied studies.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "Construct an anoxic network that produces L-alanine together with D-alanine, not enantiopure L-alanine. The ten pathways are distinct, condition-resolved graph routes, not ten independently demonstrated synthesis mechanisms. Four pyruvate-supply branches converge on iron-driven reductive amination; two Strecker entry sequences feed acidic hydrolysis, alkaline hydrolysis, or a hydantoin storage-and-release branch. The cyanohydrin-feeding and hydantoin implementations are explicitly conditional extensions beyond the supplied evidence. Unsupported sulfur-driven lactate oxidation, selective serine hydrogenolysis, and preparative acetaldehyde recovery from pyruvate decomposition are excluded. The feedstock boundary includes externally supplied acetaldehyde, oxaloacetate, HCN, ammonia, and hydroxylamine; their environmental production and accumulation are not claimed to be solved. CO2/H2 fixation supplies an alternative carbon entry from simpler molecules. Except for explicitly identified reported temperatures, numerical operating windows below are proposed evaluation conditions, not verified literature protocols or guaranteed yields. Reported pH windows refer to measurements at approximately 298 K. Neutral organic formulas represent reference protonation states; environmental acid-base speciation is implicit, while protons and iron oxidation states are explicit where needed for redox balance. Unspecified stereochemistry in precursor nodes denotes unresolved stereoisomer populations. The environment_formed field gives a representative formation environment; reaction records specify all alternatives. The schema's intermediate role also covers coproducts. Useful yields, hydroxylamine supply, mineral-specific CO2 fixation performance, and environmentally realistic hydrolysis remain limiting uncertainties.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "molecular hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid; pyruvate reference species", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C4H4O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxobutanedioic acid", "common_name": "oxaloacetic acid; oxaloacetate reference species", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "elemental iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydron", "common_name": "proton", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H5NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "lactonitrile; unresolved stereoisomers", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H5NO/c1-3(5)2-4/h3,5H,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "alaninonitrile; unresolved stereoisomers", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C4H6N2O2", "iupac_name": "5-methylimidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "5-methylhydantoin; unresolved stereoisomers", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "D-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Awaruite-mediated hydrogen-dependent CO2 fixation to pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["awaruite, Ni3Fe"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373 K; consistent with the temperature reported in the supplied review", "pressure": "Proposed combined H2 and CO2 partial pressure of 25 bar, with H2:CO2 = 4:1; water vapor contributes additional pressure", "catalyst": "awaruite, Ni3Fe", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: pH 7.5-9.5, 10-100 g/L characterized mineral, and 16-48 h residence. Maintain sufficient alkalinity under CO2 loading. Quantify mineral corrosion, carbon-containing blanks, and pyruvate formation using labeled CO2. After formation, cool and transfer locally to the amination zone rather than assuming ocean-scale concentration."}, "mechanism": "Empirical net carbon fixation: 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvic acid + 3 H2O. Alloy surfaces activate hydrogen and promote carbon reduction and coupling. A unique sequence of soluble C1 intermediates is not asserted.", "reasoning": "Preiner et al. (2020) support hydrogen-dependent mineral-mediated formation of metabolic intermediates. Awaruite is chemically justified by its occurrence in serpentinized rocks and its hydrogen-activation capability. This branch evaluates an H2-fed alloy system without silently treating added Fe0 as a renewable catalyst. The supplied summary does not establish its exact mineral-specific yield under the proposed recipe; pyruvate identity, carbon provenance, catalyst stability, and productive flux require confirmation. Pyruvate decomposition and dilution remain competing processes."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Magnetite-mediated hydrogen-dependent CO2 fixation to pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["magnetite, Fe3O4"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373 K; consistent with the temperature reported in the supplied review", "pressure": "Proposed combined H2 and CO2 partial pressure of 25 bar, with H2:CO2 = 4:1; water vapor contributes additional pressure", "catalyst": "magnetite, Fe3O4", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: pH 7.5-9.5, 10-100 g/L characterized magnetite, and 16-48 h residence. Exclude O2 and document mineral phases before and after reaction. Distinguish pyruvate from other reduced-carbon products and organic contamination. Rapid local cooling and retention are required before amination."}, "mechanism": "The same balanced net conversion as rxn_001 is evaluated on an iron-oxide surface. Surface adsorption and redox chemistry can facilitate CO2 reduction; the detailed carbon-coupling mechanism is unresolved.", "reasoning": "The supplied account of Preiner et al. (2020) identifies magnetite as an alternative mineral system. It does not justify assuming the same pyruvate yield or selectivity as awaruite, so this is a separate mineral-specific evaluation branch rather than an independent confirmation of the entire alanine pathway. Magnetite's structural Fe(III) does not require molecular oxygen in the reaction. H2 must supply the reducing equivalents assumed by the net equation; substantial mineral consumption would require revised material accounting."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Ferrous-ion-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["dissolved iron(II) ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343 K, the reported laboratory temperature; interpreted as a cool hydrothermal mixing-zone analogue", "pressure": "Proposed 10-50 bar for a shallow subsurface implementation; pressure is not established as a requirement by the supplied report", "catalyst": "iron(II) ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed evaluation window: pH 4.5-6.5, 1-10 mM oxaloacetate, 1-10 mM dissolved Fe2+, and 0.5-24 h residence. Avoid sulfide-rich or phosphate-rich carryover that removes dissolved iron. Capture pyruvate before extended heating. External oxaloacetate availability is an explicit feedstock assumption."}, "mechanism": "Lewis-acid coordination facilitates beta-keto-acid decarboxylation. The resulting enol tautomerizes to pyruvate: oxaloacetic acid -> pyruvic acid + CO2.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019) provide the strongest supplied support for this upstream conversion, including operation at 343 K with iron species. Fe2+ is justified as a carbonyl-coordinating Lewis acid, not as a stoichiometric oxidant. The proposed concentrations and residence window are not claimed as the paper's exact protocol. Oxaloacetate instability supports its conversion but also makes its accumulation and delivery a separate limitation."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Unassisted surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed evaluation window: pH 4-7, 1-10 mM oxaloacetate, and 1-7 days. Use shaded aqueous pores because UV is unnecessary and can destroy keto acids. For subsequent rxn_005, require short-distance anoxic infiltration into a cooled Fe0-bearing water-rock zone, with measured dilution and adjustment to the amination pH window."}, "mechanism": "Thermal beta-keto-acid decarboxylation followed by enol-keto tautomerization gives pyruvate and CO2 without an external redox reagent.", "reasoning": "Ritson (2021) is cited in the supplied review for oxaloacetate-to-pyruvate decarboxylation in surface chemistry. The uncatalyzed window here is a proposed implementation based on established beta-keto-acid reactivity, not a claim to reproduce the reported one-hour experiment. Sulfur and UV are not assigned catalytic roles without evidence. This branch differs from rxn_003 in catalyst requirement and setting, but represents the same underlying carbon transformation. Delivery to the iron-driven amination zone is conditional, not demonstrated."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Sacrificial-iron reduction of the pyruvate-hydroxylamine condensation product", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_005", "mol_008", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_009", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["iron(II) ions as Lewis-acid promoter; elemental iron is an explicitly consumed reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343 K, as reported for the iron-promoted amino-acid chemistry", "pressure": "Proposed 10-50 bar in a cooled subsurface water-rock zone; elevated pressure is not asserted to be necessary for the laboratory reaction", "catalyst": "iron(II) ions; Fe0 is a sacrificial reagent rather than a catalyst", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed evaluation window: pH 4.5-6.5, 1-10 mM pyruvate, 1-3 equivalents hydroxylamine, and 1-48 h residence. Supply at least the net requirement of two Fe atoms per alanine molecule, with additional iron potentially lost to corrosion. Maintain a proton reservoir, measure dissolved Fe2+, and exclude sulfide or carbonate carryover that passivates the metal. Hydroxylamine supply and competing decomposition must be measured."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate condenses with hydroxylamine to an oxime-like intermediate, followed by four-electron reduction involving C=N reduction and N-O cleavage. Per alanine molecule, the balanced reference equation is pyruvic acid + NH2OH + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> alanine + 2 Fe2+ + 2 H2O. Achiral conditions produce both alanine enantiomers.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019) support iron-promoted conversion of keto acids to amino acids using hydroxylamine. The two water-placement variants in the supplied review are not counted as separate mechanisms: without a reductant they are incomplete redox equations. Here Fe0 consumption, proton demand, and monatomic Fe2+ production are explicit. Hydroxylamine is not treated as an automatically abundant geochemical feedstock. Neither this reaction nor the supplied evidence establishes an L-enantiomeric excess."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Acetaldehyde Strecker synthesis through imine capture", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar, with restricted gas exchange", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed evaluation window: pH 8-9.5, 1-20 mM acetaldehyde, 1-50 mM total cyanide, 0.1-1 M total ammonia, and 1-7 days. Gas-retaining, optically shielded pores are required to limit acetaldehyde, HCN, and NH3 loss. These concentrated inventories require an independently justified accumulation mechanism. Subsequent acidic hydrolysis must occur in a separate pH stage."}, "mechanism": "Acetaldehyde and ammonia reversibly form an imine or iminium species. Cyanide addition gives unresolved 2-aminopropanenitrile. The net conversion releases water.", "reasoning": "Classical alanine Strecker chemistry provides direct chemical precedent (Strecker, 1850), while Patel et al. (2015) support aminonitrile-based amino-acid precursor chemistry in a prebiotic framework. Acetaldehyde is explicitly supplied rather than assumed to be selectively recovered from pyruvate degradation. The main environmental limitations are volatile-feedstock retention, ammonia concentration, competing cyanohydrin formation, and aminonitrile survival under surface UV. No mineral is assigned an unsupported catalytic function."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Reversible capture of acetaldehyde as lactonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar, with restricted gas exchange", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed evaluation window: pH 8-9, 1-20 mM acetaldehyde, 1-50 mM total cyanide, and hours to days. Retain the aqueous phase in shaded pores. Cyanide supplied by HCN dissociation is the nucleophile; no additional mineral catalyst is assumed. Measure equilibrium partitioning rather than assuming quantitative aldehyde storage."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, and proton transfer gives the cyanohydrin lactonitrile. Formation is reversible and produces an unresolved stereoisomer population.", "reasoning": "This is established cyanohydrin chemistry, but the supplied report does not directly validate this particular feed-storage implementation. It is therefore speculative as an integrated prebiotic branch; Patel et al. (2015) provide contextual support for cyanide-derived precursor chemistry, not a measured yield for this proposed step. Inclusion is justified because reversible cyanohydrin formation can change the retention and timing of a volatile aldehyde feed, rather than because it creates a nominal extra pathway."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Ammonia-driven equilibration of lactonitrile into alaninonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar, with restricted gas exchange", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed evaluation window: pH 8-9.5, 1-20 mM lactonitrile, 0.1-1 M total ammonia, and 1-7 days. Retain released HCN and acetaldehyde and shield the solution from destructive UV. Determine the lactonitrile/aminonitrile distribution experimentally; ammonia addition alone does not establish a useful conversion."}, "mechanism": "Lactonitrile reversibly releases acetaldehyde and HCN. Ammonia captures acetaldehyde through imine formation, after which cyanide addition gives alaninonitrile. The net equation is lactonitrile + NH3 -> alaninonitrile + H2O; direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group is not assumed.", "reasoning": "The chemical basis is the coupled cyanohydrin and Strecker equilibria, with classical Strecker precedent (Strecker, 1850). The supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary does not establish this exact feeding sequence, so its prebiotic implementation is speculative. This is an alternative entry through a retained precursor pool, not a new fundamental C-N bond-forming mechanism or an independent demonstration of alanine synthesis."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Acid-promoted alaninonitrile hydrolysis to racemic alanine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["hydronium ions supplied by acidic volcanic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed environmental test window: pH 1-2, 1-20 mM alaninonitrile, and 1-30 days in shaded hot-spring water. This milder acid regime is not asserted to match a concentrated-acid analytical hydrolysis protocol. Stage acidification after aminonitrile formation; quantify both alaninamide accumulation and alanine release. At reaction pH, ammonia and alanine are protonated; the graph uses neutral reference species."}, "mechanism": "Acid activation of the nitrile permits water addition, formation of alaninamide, and subsequent amide hydrolysis. Per aminonitrile molecule, two water molecules yield alanine and ammonia. Both hydrolytic transformations are included in this lumped reaction.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) support alaninonitrile as an alanine precursor. The review's ambient-aqueous description does not establish rapid, complete hydrolysis at neutral pH. Heating, acid availability, and the slower amide-cleavage stage are therefore explicit constraints. Acidic volcanic waters offer a chemically plausible catalyst source, but useful conversion in this proposed environmental window must be demonstrated. No chiral selector is present, so L- and D-alanine are both products."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Alkaline alaninonitrile hydrolysis to racemic alanine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["hydroxide supplied by concentrated alkaline water-rock fluids"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydroxide ion as base promoter", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed evaluation window: cooled-sample pH 11.5-12.5, 1-20 mM alaninonitrile, and 1-30 days. Requires unusually alkaline, retained geothermal water rather than ordinary seawater. Monitor alkalinity consumption, alaninamide accumulation, ammonia escape, and competing aminonitrile degradation. Alaninate is the environmental product; subsequent acid-base equilibration is implicit in the neutral reference representation."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide addition to the nitrile leads to an amide intermediate; further base-promoted acyl substitution produces alaninate and ammonia. Neutral-reference atom balance is alaninonitrile + 2 H2O -> alanine + NH3.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) support the precursor-to-amino-acid relationship, but the supplied review does not establish this alkaline recipe or its yield. This is a condition-dependent implementation of ordinary nitrile and amide hydrolysis, not an independent carbon-skeleton synthesis. Its chemical distinction from rxn_009 is base-promoted rather than acid-promoted hydrolysis. Strong alkalinity must be geochemically available, and failure to hydrolyze the amide efficiently would block the pathway."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Carbon dioxide capture of alaninonitrile as 5-methylhydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "323-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in a CO2-retaining aqueous pocket", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed evaluation window: pH 8-9.5, 1-20 mM alaninonitrile, 0.05-0.5 M dissolved inorganic carbon, and 1-7 days. An ammonia/carbonate reservoir buffers the solution without supplying additional net nitrogen to this step. The concentration and carbon-retention requirements are substantial. No organic cosolvent is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Aminonitrile carbamylation by CO2, intramolecular cyclization, and rearrangement generate 5-methylhydantoin through Bucherer-Bergs-type chemistry. The balanced net equation is alaninonitrile + CO2 -> 5-methylhydantoin.", "reasoning": "Classical hydantoin synthesis provides additional chemical precedent (Bucherer and Lieb, 1934), but this pathway is not supported by the supplied prebiotic studies and is explicitly speculative as a geochemical implementation. It is included because trapping a labile aminonitrile in a cyclic ureide could alter precursor persistence and release, not because hydantoin formation establishes alanine availability. Formation in water alone, competition with simple hydrolysis, and the required carbonate concentration must be tested."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Hydrothermal alkaline hydrolysis of 5-methylhydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_002", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["hydroxide supplied by hyperalkaline water-rock fluids"], "conditions": {"temperature": "423-453 K", "pressure": "Proposed 30-100 bar to retain liquid water", "catalyst": "hydroxide ion as base promoter", "aqueous": true, "other": "Speculative evaluation window: cooled-sample pH 12-13, 1-20 mM hydantoin, and 1-7 days. Require short-distance infiltration from a surface geothermal pocket into a retained alkaline subsurface fluid, not assumed transport from oceanic vents to surface pools. Measure N-carbamoylalanine accumulation, total alanine yield, and thermal destruction. These conditions are demanding and may not provide useful selectivity."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic ring opening forms N-carbamoylalanine; subsequent carbamoyl cleavage releases alanine, ammonia, and inorganic carbon. Per hydantoin molecule, the neutral-reference balance is 5-methylhydantoin + 2 H2O -> alanine + NH3 + CO2.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin-to-amino-acid hydrolysis is established preparative chemistry associated with hydantoin synthesis, but the supplied research provides no validation of this environmental implementation. This reaction is explicitly speculative and is the principal bottleneck of the hydantoin branches. Classical chemical precedent, including Bucherer and Lieb (1934), does not establish selective hydrolysis in natural hyperalkaline fluids. Alanine destruction, incomplete carbamoyl cleavage, alkalinity availability, and transport could each prevent productive flux."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Awaruite CO2 fixation followed by iron-driven amination", "description": "CO2 and H2 supply pyruvate on an alloy surface; hydroxylamine and sacrificial Fe0 then furnish racemic alanine containing the L target. Mineral-specific pyruvate production, local retention, and hydroxylamine supply are required. The two stages need different pH conditions.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Magnetite CO2 fixation followed by iron-driven amination", "description": "An oxide-mineral alternative supplies the same pyruvate intermediate for the common amination step. This is a catalyst-distinct graph route, not evidence that magnetite and awaruite have equal yields or that the complete sequence has been demonstrated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Ferrous oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by amination", "description": "Externally supplied oxaloacetate undergoes Fe2+-promoted decarboxylation before the supported hydroxylamine/Fe0 amination chemistry. This is among the strongest supplied reaction combinations, but oxaloacetate and hydroxylamine availability remain outside the demonstrated network boundary.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Surface oxaloacetate decay followed by subsurface amination", "description": "Unassisted surface decarboxylation supplies pyruvate, which must enter a nearby cooled Fe0-bearing subsurface zone without excessive dilution or degradation. The transfer, pH staging, and metal-passivation constraints are explicit prerequisites rather than demonstrated processes.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Direct Strecker entry with acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Acetaldehyde, ammonia, and HCN form alaninonitrile through imine capture, followed by a separate acidic hot-spring hydrolysis stage. Both nitrile hydration and amide cleavage must proceed; the product is racemic alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Direct Strecker entry with alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "The same Strecker precursor is processed in an alkaline geothermal water rather than an acidic pool. This is a hydrolysis-condition alternative to P5, not an independent amino-acid carbon-skeleton synthesis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Direct Strecker entry with hydantoin storage and release", "description": "Alaninonitrile is conditionally captured with CO2 as 5-methylhydantoin before hydrothermal hydrolysis. The hydantoin formation and release implementation is speculative; its value would depend on improved precursor persistence outweighing the demanding release conditions.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Cyanohydrin-fed Strecker entry with acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Acetaldehyde first enters a reversible lactonitrile pool. Ammonia-driven equilibration supplies alaninonitrile for acidic hydrolysis. The cyanohydrin-feeding implementation is conditional and shares the fundamental Strecker bond-forming chemistry with P5.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Cyanohydrin-fed Strecker entry with alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "A retained lactonitrile pool feeds alaninonitrile formation, followed by alkaline hydrolysis. Productive flux requires favorable coupled equilibria, volatile retention, and sufficient alkalinity for both hydrolytic stages.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Cyanohydrin-fed hydantoin storage-and-release pathway", "description": "Lactonitrile feeds alaninonitrile, which is captured as a hydantoin and subsequently hydrolyzed. This is the most conditional route: neither the combined feed-storage sequence nor selective environmental hydantoin release is established by the supplied studies.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_015"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_014", "mol_016", "mol_017"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_001", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_012"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_002", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_011"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_004", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_008"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_006", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_014", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_016", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_017", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 554276.8199443817, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T05:26:06.501435", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct an anoxic network that produces L-alanine together with D-alanine, not enantiopure L-alanine. The ten pathways are distinct, condition-resolved graph routes, not ten independently demonstrated synthesis mechanisms. Four pyruvate-supply branches converge on iron-driven reductive amination; two Strecker entry sequences feed acidic hydrolysis, alkaline hydrolysis, or a hydantoin storage-and-release branch. The cyanohydrin-feeding and hydantoin implementations are explicitly conditional extensions beyond the supplied evidence. Unsupported sulfur-driven lactate oxidation, selective serine hydrogenolysis, and preparative acetaldehyde recovery from pyruvate decomposition are excluded. The feedstock boundary includes externally supplied acetaldehyde, oxaloacetate, HCN, ammonia, and hydroxylamine; their environmental production and accumulation are not claimed to be solved. CO2/H2 fixation supplies an alternative carbon entry from simpler molecules. Except for explicitly identified reported temperatures, numerical operating windows below are proposed evaluation conditions, not verified literature protocols or guaranteed yields. Reported pH windows refer to measurements at approximately 298 K. Neutral organic formulas represent reference protonation states; environmental acid-base speciation is implicit, while protons and iron oxidation states are explicit where needed for redox balance. Unspecified stereochemistry in precursor nodes denotes unresolved stereoisomer populations. The environment_formed field gives a representative formation environment; reaction records specify all alternatives. The schema's intermediate role also covers coproducts. Useful yields, hydroxylamine supply, mineral-specific CO2 fixation performance, and environmentally realistic hydrolysis remain limiting uncertainties.\n\nMOLECULES (17 nodes):\n  - mol_001: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_002: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2 (molecular hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H3N (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_005: H3NO (hydroxylamine) [starting]\n  - mol_006: C3H4O3 (pyruvic acid; pyruvate reference species) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C4H4O5 (oxaloacetic acid; oxaloacetate reference species) [starting]\n  - mol_008: Fe (elemental iron) [starting]\n  - mol_009: Fe+2 (ferrous ion) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: H+ (proton) [starting]\n  - mol_011: C2H4O (acetaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_012: CHN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_013: C3H5NO (lactonitrile; unresolved stereoisomers) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C3H6N2 (alaninonitrile; unresolved stereoisomers) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C4H6N2O2 (5-methylhydantoin; unresolved stereoisomers) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C3H7NO2 (L-alanine) [target]\n  - mol_017: C3H7NO2 (D-alanine) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (12 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_006, mol_001 (Awaruite-mediated hydrogen-dependent CO2 fixation to pyruvate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_002: mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_006, mol_001 (Magnetite-mediated hydrogen-dependent CO2 fixation to pyruvate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_003: mol_007 -> mol_006, mol_002 (Ferrous-ion-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_004: mol_007 -> mol_006, mol_002 (Unassisted surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_006, mol_005, mol_008, mol_010 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_009, mol_001 (Sacrificial-iron reduction of the pyruvate-hydroxylamine condensation product) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_006: mol_011, mol_004, mol_012 -> mol_014, mol_001 (Acetaldehyde Strecker synthesis through imine capture) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_011, mol_012 -> mol_013 (Reversible capture of acetaldehyde as lactonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_013, mol_004 -> mol_014, mol_001 (Ammonia-driven equilibration of lactonitrile into alaninonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_014, mol_001 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_004 (Acid-promoted alaninonitrile hydrolysis to racemic alanine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_014, mol_001 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_004 (Alkaline alaninonitrile hydrolysis to racemic alanine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_014, mol_002 -> mol_015 (Carbon dioxide capture of alaninonitrile as 5-methylhydantoin) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_015, mol_001 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_002, mol_004 (Hydrothermal alkaline hydrolysis of 5-methylhydantoin) [Hydrothermal]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Awaruite CO2 fixation followed by iron-driven amination (2 steps)\n  - P2: Magnetite CO2 fixation followed by iron-driven amination (2 steps)\n  - P3: Ferrous oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by amination (2 steps)\n  - P4: Surface oxaloacetate decay followed by subsurface amination (2 steps)\n  - P5: Direct Strecker entry with acidic hydrolysis (2 steps)\n  - P6: Direct Strecker entry with alkaline hydrolysis (2 steps)\n  - P7: Direct Strecker entry with hydantoin storage and release (3 steps)\n  - P8: Cyanohydrin-fed Strecker entry with acidic hydrolysis (3 steps)\n  - P9: Cyanohydrin-fed Strecker entry with alkaline hydrolysis (3 steps)\n  - P10: Cyanohydrin-fed hydantoin storage-and-release pathway (4 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 12 reactions | 6 balanced as written | 6 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_001 balanceable: 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> C3H4O3 + 3 H2O\n  - rxn_002 balanceable: 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> C3H4O3 + 3 H2O\n  - rxn_005 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 H3NO + 4 Fe + 8 H+ -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 4 Fe+2 + 4 H2O\n  - rxn_009 balanceable: 2 C3H6N2 + 4 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H3N\n  - rxn_010 balanceable: 2 C3H6N2 + 4 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H3N\n  - rxn_012 balanceable: 2 C4H6N2O2 + 4 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 CO2 + 2 H3N\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Alanine: Comprehensive Literature Review\n\n## 1. Known Prebiotic Synthesis Routes\n\nFour experimentally demonstrated pathways produce L-alanine (C3H7NO2, SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)) from prebiotically available precursors. These span hydrothermal, surface, and hybrid environments.\n\n### Pathway 1: Cyanosulfidic Nitrile Hydrolysis (Surface Environment)\n\nThe cyanosulfidic protometabolism pathway demonstrated by Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) shows that **2-aminopropanenitrile** (C3H6N2, SMILES: CC(C#N)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3) undergoes hydrolysis in aqueous conditions to yield L-alanine and ammonia.\n\n**Reaction**: 2-aminopropanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Alanine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of water to the nitrile group of 2-aminopropanenitrile, forming an amide intermediate, followed by further hydrolysis to the carboxylic acid (alanine) with release of ammonia (NH3, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3).\n\nThis is part of the broader Powell-Sutherland cyanosulfidic chemistry framework, in which HCN-derived nitriles serve as amino acid precursors via hydrolysis under wet-dry cycling or UV-irradiated surface pool conditions.\n\n### Pathway 2: Reductive Amination of Pyruvate with Hydroxylamine (Hydrothermal, Iron-Catalyzed)\n\nMuchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019, *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) demonstrated that **pyruvate** (C3H4O3, SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)) reacts with **hydroxylamine** (H3NO, SMILES: NO, InChI: InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2) in the presence of elemental iron (Fe\u2070) and ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a) to produce L-alanine.\n\nTwo variant conditions were reported:\n\n**Reaction A**: Pyruvate + hydroxylamine + H2O \u2192 L-Alanine (with water as co-substrate)\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO.O>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070, [Fe]) and **ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a, [Fe2+])\n- Mechanism: Iron-promoted reductive amination \u2014 hydroxylamine acts as the nitrogen donor, and iron species provide the reducing equivalents to drive the transamination. The \u03b1-keto acid (pyruvate) is converted to the corresponding \u03b1-amino acid (alanine) at mild hydrothermal temperatures.\n\n**Reaction B**: Pyruvate + hydroxylamine \u2192 L-Alanine + H2O (net dehydration)\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO>>CC(N)C(=O)O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070) and **ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n- Mechanism: Same iron-promoted reductive amination as Reaction A but with net water release. This represents the overall thermodynamic balance of the transformation.\n\nThese reactions are central to the Muchowska et al. demonstration that a simple iron-driven reaction network can interconvert universal metabolic precursors (\u03b1-keto acids \u2194 \u03b1-amino acids) under mild aqueous conditions with metallic iron.\n\n### Pathway 3: Hydrothermal Reduction of L-Serine (Hydrothermal)\n\nAubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2) showed that **L-serine** (C3H7NO3, SMILES: C(C(C(=O)O)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)) can be reduced to L-alanine by molecular hydrogen under submarine hydrothermal conditions.\n\n**Reaction**: L-Serine + H2 \u2192 L-Alanine\n- SMILES: N[C@H](CO)C(=O)O.[HH]>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Conditions: Elevated temperature and pressure characteristic of submarine hydrothermal vents\n- Mechanism: Reductive dehydroxylation \u2014 the hydroxymethyl group (-CH2OH) of serine is reduced to a methyl group (-CH3), converting serine to alanine. Molecular hydrogen (H2, SMILES: [HH]) serves as the reductant, which is abundantly available in hydrothermal vent fluids from serpentinization.\n\nThis pathway links alanine synthesis to the broader amino acid inventory of hydrothermal systems, where serine (itself formed via other prebiotic routes) undergoes reductive transformation.\n\n### Pathway 4: CO2 Fixation to Pyruvate via Fischer-Tropsch-Type Chemistry (Hydrothermal)\n\nPreiner, Igarashi, Muchowska, Yu, Varma, Kleinermanns, Nobu, Kamagata, T\u00fcys\u00fcz, Moran, and Martin (2020, *Nature Ecology & Evolution*, DOI: 10.1038/s41559-020-1125-6) demonstrated that **CO2** and **H2** can be converted directly to pyruvate (and water) on native iron and nickel-iron minerals at 100\u00b0C, providing an abiotic route to this critical metabolic hub molecule.\n\n**Reaction**: 3 CO2 + 5 H2 \u2192 Pyruvate + 3 H2O\n- SMILES: [HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].O=C=O.O=C=O.O=C=O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 100\u00b0C (373 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070, [Fe]), **awaruite** (Ni3Fe), and **magnetite** (Fe3O4, [O-2].[O-2].[O-2].[O-2].[Fe+2].[Fe+3].[Fe+3])\n- Conditions reported under three catalyst combinations:\n  - (a) Elemental Iron + awaruite at 100\u00b0C\n  - (b) Elemental Iron + magnetite at 100\u00b0C\n  - (c) Magnetite alone at 100\u00b0C\n- Mechanism: H2-dependent CO2 reduction on native metal surfaces \u2014 analogous to Fischer-Tropsch-type synthesis. Iron and nickel-iron alloy (awaruite) surfaces activate H2 and reduce CO2 stepwise through C1 intermediates (formate, formaldehyde, CO) to C2 (acetate) and then C3 (pyruvate) products. Magnetite provides an oxide surface that facilitates CO2 adsorption and reduction.\n\nThis is a key reaction because it connects the simplest geochemical feedstocks (CO2 and H2, both abundant in hydrothermal vent environments from serpentinization) to pyruvate, the central metabolic hub for alanine synthesis.\n\n## 2. Key Intermediates and Precursors\n\n### Hub Molecule: Pyruvate (C3H4O3)\n- Formula: C3H4O3\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\n- MW: 88.06\n- Role: **Central metabolic hub** \u2014 pyruvate is the key convergence point in the network. It is produced by at least four different upstream reactions (CO2 fixation, oxaloacetate decarboxylation, lactic acid oxidation, cyanosulfidic decarboxylation) and consumed by the reductive amination reactions that produce alanine. Pyruvate is a universal metabolic precursor in extant biology and its prebiotic availability is critical.\n\n### Upstream Precursors to Pyruvate\n\n**Oxaloacetate** (C4H4O5)\n- Formula: C4H4O5\n- SMILES: C(C(=O)C(=O)O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)\n- MW: 132.07\n- Role: Intermediate \u2014 a C4 dicarboxylic \u03b1-keto acid that undergoes decarboxylation to pyruvate\n\n**Lactic acid** (C3H6O3)\n- Formula: C3H6O3\n- SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)\n- MW: 90.08\n- Role: Intermediate \u2014 oxidized to pyruvate under cyanosulfidic conditions\n\n### Other Key Molecules\n\n**Hydroxylamine** (H3NO)\n- Formula: H3NO\n- SMILES: NO\n- InChI: InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\n- MW: 33.03\n- Role: Nitrogen donor for reductive amination of pyruvate to alanine\n\n**2-Aminopropanenitrile** (C3H6N2)\n- Formula: C3H6N2\n- SMILES: CC(C#N)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3\n- MW: 70.09\n- Role: Aminonitrile intermediate \u2014 Strecker-type precursor that hydrolyzes directly to alanine\n\n**L-Serine** (C3H7NO3)\n- Formula: C3H7NO3\n- SMILES: C(C(C(=O)O)N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)\n- MW: 105.09\n- Role: Amino acid precursor \u2014 reduced to alanine under hydrothermal conditions\n\n### Starting Molecules\n- **H2O**: SMILES: O, InChI: InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\n- **CO2**: SMILES: C(=O)=O, InChI: InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\n- **H2** (molecular hydrogen): SMILES: [HH], InChI: InChI=1S/H2/h1H\n- **NH3** (ammonia): SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\n\n## 3. Reaction Mechanisms and Conditions\n\n### Reactions Producing Pyruvate (Upstream)\n\n**Reaction U1: Oxaloacetate decarboxylation (iron-catalyzed)**\n- Oxaloacetate \u2192 Pyruvate + CO2\n- SMILES: O=C(O)CC(=O)C(=O)O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].O=C=O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: Ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n- Source: Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n- Mechanism: \u03b2-decarboxylation of oxaloacetate catalyzed by Fe\u00b2\u207a ions. This is thermodynamically favorable and proceeds readily at mild temperatures. Iron(II) acts as a Lewis acid to coordinate the \u03b2-keto acid and facilitate CO2 loss.\n\n**Reaction U2: Oxaloacetate decarboxylation (cyanosulfidic)**\n- Oxaloacetate \u2192 Pyruvate\n- SMILES: C(C(=O)C(=O)[O-])C(=O)[O-]>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-]\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Time: 1 hour\n- Source: Ritson (2021), *Science Advances*, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.abh3981\n- Mechanism: Decarboxylation under cyanosulfidic conditions as part of a prebiotic analog of the Krebs cycle.\n\n**Reaction U3: Lactic acid oxidation (cyanosulfidic)**\n- Lactic acid \u2192 Pyruvate\n- SMILES: CC(C(=O)[O-])O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-]\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Ritson (2021), *Science Advances*, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.abh3981\n- Mechanism: Oxidation of the \u03b1-hydroxy group of lactic acid to a ketone, converting lactate to pyruvate. This occurs under cyanosulfidic conditions where sulfur species act as oxidants.\n\n**Reaction U4: CO2 + H2 fixation to pyruvate (Fischer-Tropsch-type)**\n- 3 CO2 + 5 H2 \u2192 Pyruvate + 3 H2O\n- SMILES: [HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].O=C=O.O=C=O.O=C=O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 100\u00b0C (373 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: Elemental Iron (Fe\u2070), awaruite (Ni3Fe), magnetite (Fe3O4)\n- Source: Preiner, Igarashi, Muchowska, Yu, Varma, Kleinermanns, Nobu, Kamagata, T\u00fcys\u00fcz, Moran, and Martin (2020), *Nature Ecology & Evolution*, DOI: 10.1038/s41559-020-1125-6\n\n### Reactions Producing L-Alanine (Final Steps)\n\n**Reaction F1: Nitrile hydrolysis**\n- 2-aminopropanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Alanine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n\n**Reaction F2: Reductive amination (variant A)**\n- Pyruvate + hydroxylamine + H2O \u2192 L-Alanine\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO.O>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal, 70\u00b0C, Fe\u2070 + Fe\u00b2\u207a\n- Source: Muchowska et al. (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n\n**Reaction F3: Reductive amination (variant B)**\n- Pyruvate + hydroxylamine \u2192 L-Alanine + 2 H2O\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO>>CC(N)C(=O)O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal, 70\u00b0C, Fe\u2070 + Fe\u00b2\u207a\n- Source: Muchowska et al. (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n\n**Reaction F4: Serine reduction**\n- L-Serine + H2 \u2192 L-Alanine\n- SMILES: N[C@H](CO)C(=O)O.[HH]>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Source: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n\n## 4. Mineral Catalysts and Surfaces\n\nThe following mineral catalysts and metal species are experimentally documented for L-alanine synthesis reactions:\n\n- **Elemental Iron (Fe\u2070)**: Highly reactive, insoluble metallic iron. Acts as a strong reductant for CO2 fixation and drives reductive amination of pyruvate. Used in: pyruvate-to-alanine reductive amination (Muchowska et al., 2019) and CO2-to-pyruvate fixation (Preiner et al., 2020).\n\n- **Ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a)**: Soluble iron(II) species, abundant in anoxic early Earth oceans. Converts to insoluble Fe\u00b3\u207a in the presence of oxygen (not relevant on anoxic early Earth). Acts as Lewis acid catalyst for decarboxylation reactions and co-catalyst for reductive amination. Used in: oxaloacetate decarboxylation (Muchowska et al., 2019) and pyruvate-to-alanine reductive amination (Muchowska et al., 2019).\n\n- **Awaruite (Ni3Fe)**: A nickel-iron alloy mineral found in serpentinized ultramafic rocks. Provides catalytic surface for H2 activation and CO2 reduction. Used in: CO2-to-pyruvate Fischer-Tropsch-type synthesis (Preiner et al., 2020).\n\n- **Magnetite (Fe3O4)**: A mixed-valence iron oxide mineral common in hydrothermal systems. Facilitates CO2 adsorption and stepwise reduction. Used in: CO2-to-pyruvate synthesis (Preiner et al., 2020).\n\n## 5. Experimental Evidence\n\n### Study 1: Patel et al. (2015)\n- **Title**: \"Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Chemistry\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a unified cyanosulfidic chemistry starting from HCN and H2S can produce precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids. For alanine specifically, 2-aminopropanenitrile is formed via cyanosulfidic Strecker-like chemistry and hydrolyzes to alanine in aqueous conditions.\n\n### Study 2: Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019)\n- **Title**: \"Synthesis and breakdown of universal metabolic precursors promoted by iron\"\n- **Journal**: Nature\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that metallic iron (Fe\u2070) and ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a) at 70\u00b0C in water promote an interconnected network of reactions analogous to the core of biological metabolism. This includes: (a) interconversion of \u03b1-keto acids and \u03b1-amino acids via reductive amination with hydroxylamine, specifically pyruvate \u2192 alanine; (b) decarboxylation of oxaloacetate to pyruvate. This establishes a simple geochemical analog of the reductive TCA cycle and amino acid biosynthesis.\n\n### Study 3: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008)\n- **Title**: \"The Role of Submarine Hydrothermal Systems in the Synthesis of Amino Acids\"\n- **Journal**: Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres\n- **DOI**: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n- **Key finding**: Investigated amino acid synthesis under simulated submarine hydrothermal vent conditions. Demonstrated that L-serine can be reduced to L-alanine by molecular hydrogen, showing that hydrothermal processing can interconvert amino acids and expand the prebiotic amino acid inventory.\n\n### Study 4: Preiner et al. (2020)\n- **Title**: \"A hydrogen-dependent geochemical analogue of primordial carbon and energy metabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Ecology & Evolution\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/s41559-020-1125-6\n- **Key finding**: Showed that CO2 and H2 \u2014 the two simplest geochemical feedstocks available at serpentinizing hydrothermal vents \u2014 react on native iron, awaruite (Ni3Fe), and magnetite (Fe3O4) at 100\u00b0C to produce pyruvate and other metabolic intermediates. This provides a purely geochemical route from CO2 to the central metabolic hub (pyruvate) from which amino acids including alanine can be formed.\n\n### Study 5: Ritson (2021)\n- **Title**: \"A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle\"\n- **Journal**: Science Advances\n- **DOI**: 10.1126/sciadv.abh3981\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a cyanosulfidic reaction network can produce the intermediates of the Krebs (TCA) cycle abiotically. Relevant to alanine synthesis: (a) oxaloacetate undergoes decarboxylation to pyruvate under cyanosulfidic conditions; (b) lactic acid is oxidized to pyruvate. Both reactions provide additional prebiotic routes to the pyruvate hub molecule.\n\n## 6. Environmental Context\n\n### Hydrothermal Environment (Primary)\nThe majority of experimentally demonstrated alanine synthesis routes operate under hydrothermal conditions:\n- **Iron-catalyzed reductive amination** (Muchowska et al., 2019): Pyruvate + hydroxylamine \u2192 alanine at 70\u00b0C with Fe\u2070/Fe\u00b2\u207a. This mimics conditions at alkaline hydrothermal vents where metallic iron and dissolved Fe\u00b2\u207a are available from serpentinization, and temperatures of 70\u00b0C are found in the mixing zones between hot vent fluid and cooler ocean water.\n- **CO2 fixation to pyruvate** (Preiner et al., 2020): CO2 + H2 \u2192 pyruvate at 100\u00b0C on iron/awaruite/magnetite. Serpentinization of ultramafic rock produces abundant H2 and exposes native iron and nickel-iron alloys, making these minerals and conditions naturally co-located.\n- **Serine reduction** (Aubrey et al., 2008): L-serine + H2 \u2192 alanine under submarine hydrothermal conditions, where H2 from serpentinization provides the reductant.\n- **Oxaloacetate decarboxylation** (Muchowska et al., 2019): Fe\u00b2\u207a-catalyzed at 70\u00b0C.\n\n### Surface Environment (Secondary)\n- **Cyanosulfidic nitrile hydrolysis** (Patel et al., 2015): 2-aminopropanenitrile + H2O \u2192 alanine. This operates in surface pools where HCN-derived chemistry (UV-driven, evaporitic) produces aminonitrile intermediates that hydrolyze in wet phases.\n- **Cyanosulfidic Krebs cycle analogs** (Ritson, 2021): Lactic acid \u2192 pyruvate and oxaloacetate \u2192 pyruvate under surface cyanosulfidic conditions, feeding the pyruvate pool that is then available for transamination in environments where iron is present.\n\n### Environmental Interconnection\nThe synthesis network exhibits clear cross-environment flow:\n1. **Hydrothermal \u2192 Surface**: Pyruvate and other metabolic intermediates generated at hydrothermal vents could be transported to surface environments via ocean circulation.\n2. **Surface \u2192 Hydrothermal**: Cyanosulfidic products (aminonitriles, lactic acid, oxaloacetate) generated in surface pools could be washed into hydrothermal zones.\n3. **Both \u2192 Biochemical**: Alanine produced by any pathway enters the prebiotic amino acid pool available for peptide bond formation and proto-metabolic assembly.\n\n## 7. Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **Chirality**: All demonstrated prebiotic routes produce racemic alanine (equal L and D). The origin of homochirality (biological preference for L-amino acids) remains unresolved. Mineral surface adsorption or photolysis with circularly polarized light have been proposed.\n\n2. **Hydroxylamine availability**: The Muchowska et al. pathway requires hydroxylamine (NH2OH), whose prebiotic abundance is debated. It could form from NO reduction by Fe\u2070 or from NH3 oxidation, but quantitative constraints are needed.\n\n3. **Concentration problem**: Hydrothermal vent products are diluted in the ocean. Mechanisms for concentrating pyruvate and other intermediates (evaporitic pools, mineral adsorption, thermophoresis) are important for downstream reactions.\n\n4. **2-Aminopropanenitrile stability**: The Patel et al. pathway requires this intermediate to persist long enough for hydrolysis. Its stability under UV irradiation and varying pH conditions needs further study.\n\n5. **Pyruvate stability**: Pyruvate is thermally labile and can decarboxylate to acetaldehyde + CO2 at elevated temperatures. The balance between pyruvate production and decomposition at hydrothermal temperatures is a key kinetic question.\n\n=== END CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH ===\n\nConsider how well the network aligns with this literature in your evaluation.\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\",\n            \"approved\": <true if confidence >= 0.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote: \"environmental_progression\" scores Environmental Consistency (conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "r