tion-dependent.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Identify whether the supporting experiment used magnetite alone or additional metallic iron, and report its pyruvate yield and operating conditions.\",\n        \"Establish catalyst-phase stability and demonstrate that the fixation mixture permits subsequent oxime formation and reduction.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.94\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.95,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.96,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.82,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.94,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.89,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is the strongest supported route within the declared feedstock boundary. Oxaloacetate decarboxylation is chemically favorable, and the supplied Muchowska et al. evidence supports both iron-promoted decarboxylation and pyruvate-to-alanine conversion. rxn_005 correctly supplies the four-electron reduction through consumption of two Fe0 atoms per alanine. Remaining uncertainties concern operating conditions, iron availability and productive yield rather than an identified reaction flaw.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify dissolved Fe2+, Fe0 loading, pH, temperature, concentrations and residence times for both stages.\",\n        \"Measure pyruvate and alanine yields under sequential conditions, including competing oxaloacetate or hydroxylamine consumption and iron passivation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.94\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.93,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.92,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.69,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.91,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n      \"feedback\": \"Unassisted oxaloacetate decarboxylation is credible from its beta-ketoacid chemistry, and the downstream amination is well motivated. The principal weakness is the unspecified physical connection between surface pyruvate formation and subsurface reduction. The environmental deduction concerns retention, dilution and chemical survival during transfer, not the direction or number of environments.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Define a physically plausible connection, such as infiltration into a nearby reactive fracture system, with transport times and dilution estimates.\",\n        \"Compare pyruvate delivery rates with its degradation and competing-reaction rates under the proposed transfer conditions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.95,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.77,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.92,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.87,\n      \"feedback\": \"Acetaldehyde Strecker synthesis followed by acidic aminonitrile hydrolysis is an established chemical route to racemic alanine. The supplied Patel et al. discussion supports the relevant precursor chemistry, but it does not establish an arbitrary surface hydrolysis protocol or rapid hydrolysis in ambient water. Acid strength, temperature, residence time and complete conversion beyond the amide intermediate remain unspecified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify and justify the environmental acid source, pH, temperature and hydrolysis duration.\",\n        \"Separate cyanide capture from acidification operationally, and quantify alanine yield, amide accumulation and volatile-feedstock losses.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.86\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.9,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.75,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.91,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.86,\n      \"feedback\": \"The Strecker entry is strong, and alkaline nitrile hydrolysis can produce alanine without a conservation problem. Its effectiveness in the stated surface environment is not established by a reaction label alone: substantial alkalinity, heating or long residence times may be necessary. The neutral reference formulas are acceptable; actual alaninate and ammonia speciation must nevertheless inform the operating conditions.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Provide a defensible alkaline-fluid source and a measured pH-temperature-time window for complete alaninonitrile hydrolysis.\",\n        \"Determine alanine yield relative to residual nitrile, alaninamide and decomposition products under the proposed conditions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.73,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.73\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.78,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.85,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.65,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.78,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.75,\n      \"feedback\": \"The hydantoin branch is recognizable Bucherer-Bergs-related chemistry, and both proposed net transformations are balanced. However, the supplied studies do not establish alaninonitrile capture as 5-methylhydantoin under the proposed surface conditions or complete release of alanine under environmentally realistic alkaline hydrothermal conditions. Ring opening can leave an N-carbamoyl amino acid, so ring opening alone does not demonstrate target formation.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Add direct supporting experiments or test hydantoin formation as a function of CO2 activity, ammonia, pH and temperature.\",\n        \"Demonstrate complete conversion through N-carbamoylalanine to alanine and justify hydantoin transport, alkalinity and required residence time.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.86,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.9,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.74,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.88,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.83,\n      \"feedback\": \"Reversible lactonitrile formation is chemically sound. rxn_008 is plausible as equilibration through regenerated acetaldehyde followed by imine formation and cyanide capture, rather than an assumed facile direct substitution of hydroxyl by ammonia. Its productive rate and equilibrium position are not established by the net equation. Acidic hydrolysis then offers a valid but operationally unspecified route to racemic alanine.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure alaninonitrile production from lactonitrile at defined ammonia, cyanide, pH and temperature, including competing equilibria.\",\n        \"Demonstrate a staged acidic hydrolysis protocol with complete conversion and quantified retention of carbon and nitrogen.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.86\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.89,\n      \"env