{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T05:29:33.543283", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: L-Alanine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof L-Alanine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Alanine: Comprehensive Literature Review\n\n## 1. Known Prebiotic Synthesis Routes\n\nFour experimentally demonstrated pathways produce L-alanine (C3H7NO2, SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)) from prebiotically available precursors. These span hydrothermal, surface, and hybrid environments.\n\n### Pathway 1: Cyanosulfidic Nitrile Hydrolysis (Surface Environment)\n\nThe cyanosulfidic protometabolism pathway demonstrated by Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) shows that **2-aminopropanenitrile** (C3H6N2, SMILES: CC(C#N)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3) undergoes hydrolysis in aqueous conditions to yield L-alanine and ammonia.\n\n**Reaction**: 2-aminopropanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Alanine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of water to the nitrile group of 2-aminopropanenitrile, forming an amide intermediate, followed by further hydrolysis to the carboxylic acid (alanine) with release of ammonia (NH3, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3).\n\nThis is part of the broader Powell-Sutherland cyanosulfidic chemistry framework, in which HCN-derived nitriles serve as amino acid precursors via hydrolysis under wet-dry cycling or UV-irradiated surface pool conditions.\n\n### Pathway 2: Reductive Amination of Pyruvate with Hydroxylamine (Hydrothermal, Iron-Catalyzed)\n\nMuchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019, *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) demonstrated that **pyruvate** (C3H4O3, SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)) reacts with **hydroxylamine** (H3NO, SMILES: NO, InChI: InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2) in the presence of elemental iron (Fe\u2070) and ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a) to produce L-alanine.\n\nTwo variant conditions were reported:\n\n**Reaction A**: Pyruvate + hydroxylamine + H2O \u2192 L-Alanine (with water as co-substrate)\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO.O>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070, [Fe]) and **ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a, [Fe2+])\n- Mechanism: Iron-promoted reductive amination \u2014 hydroxylamine acts as the nitrogen donor, and iron species provide the reducing equivalents to drive the transamination. The \u03b1-keto acid (pyruvate) is converted to the corresponding \u03b1-amino acid (alanine) at mild hydrothermal temperatures.\n\n**Reaction B**: Pyruvate + hydroxylamine \u2192 L-Alanine + H2O (net dehydration)\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO>>CC(N)C(=O)O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070) and **ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n- Mechanism: Same iron-promoted reductive amination as Reaction A but with net water release. This represents the overall thermodynamic balance of the transformation.\n\nThese reactions are central to the Muchowska et al. demonstration that a simple iron-driven reaction network can interconvert universal metabolic precursors (\u03b1-keto acids \u2194 \u03b1-amino acids) under mild aqueous conditions with metallic iron.\n\n### Pathway 3: Hydrothermal Reduction of L-Serine (Hydrothermal)\n\nAubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2) showed that **L-serine** (C3H7NO3, SMILES: C(C(C(=O)O)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)) can be reduced to L-alanine by molecular hydrogen under submarine hydrothermal conditions.\n\n**Reaction**: L-Serine + H2 \u2192 L-Alanine\n- SMILES: N[C@H](CO)C(=O)O.[HH]>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Conditions: Elevated temperature and pressure characteristic of submarine hydrothermal vents\n- Mechanism: Reductive dehydroxylation \u2014 the hydroxymethyl group (-CH2OH) of serine is reduced to a methyl group (-CH3), converting serine to alanine. Molecular hydrogen (H2, SMILES: [HH]) serves as the reductant, which is abundantly available in hydrothermal vent fluids from serpentinization.\n\nThis pathway links alanine synthesis to the broader amino acid inventory of hydrothermal systems, where serine (itself formed via other prebiotic routes) undergoes reductive transformation.\n\n### Pathway 4: CO2 Fixation to Pyruvate via Fischer-Tropsch-Type Chemistry (Hydrothermal)\n\nPreiner, Igarashi, Muchowska, Yu, Varma, Kleinermanns, Nobu, Kamagata, T\u00fcys\u00fcz, Moran, and Martin (2020, *Nature Ecology & Evolution*, DOI: 10.1038/s41559-020-1125-6) demonstrated that **CO2** and **H2** can be converted directly to pyruvate (and water) on native iron and nickel-iron minerals at 100\u00b0C, providing an abiotic route to this critical metabolic hub molecule.\n\n**Reaction**: 3 CO2 + 5 H2 \u2192 Pyruvate + 3 H2O\n- SMILES: [HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].O=C=O.O=C=O.O=C=O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 100\u00b0C (373 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070, [Fe]), **awaruite** (Ni3Fe), and **magnetite** (Fe3O4, [O-2].[O-2].[O-2].[O-2].[Fe+2].[Fe+3].[Fe+3])\n- Conditions reported under three catalyst combinations:\n  - (a) Elemental Iron + awaruite at 100\u00b0C\n  - (b) Elemental Iron + magnetite at 100\u00b0C\n  - (c) Magnetite alone at 100\u00b0C\n- Mechanism: H2-dependent CO2 reduction on native metal surfaces \u2014 analogous to Fischer-Tropsch-type synthesis. Iron and nickel-iron alloy (awaruite) surfaces activate H2 and reduce CO2 stepwise through C1 intermediates (formate, formaldehyde, CO) to C2 (acetate) and then C3 (pyruvate) products. Magnetite provides an oxide surface that facilitates CO2 adsorption and reduction.\n\nThis is a key reaction because it connects the simplest geochemical feedstocks (CO2 and H2, both abundant in hydrothermal vent environments from serpentinization) to pyruvate, the central metabolic hub for alanine synthesis.\n\n## 2. Key Intermediates and Precursors\n\n### Hub Molecule: Pyruvate (C3H4O3)\n- Formula: C3H4O3\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\n- MW: 88.06\n- Role: **Central metabolic hub** \u2014 pyruvate is the key convergence point in the network. It is produced by at least four different upstream reactions (CO2 fixation, oxaloacetate decarboxylation, lactic acid oxidation, cyanosulfidic decarboxylation) and consumed by the reductive amination reactions that produce alanine. Pyruvate is a universal metabolic precursor in extant biology and its prebiotic availability is critical.\n\n### Upstream Precursors to Pyruvate\n\n**Oxaloacetate** (C4H4O5)\n- Formula: C4H4O5\n- SMILES: C(C(=O)C(=O)O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)\n- MW: 132.07\n- Role: Intermediate \u2014 a C4 dicarboxylic \u03b1-keto acid that undergoes decarboxylation to pyruvate\n\n**Lactic acid** (C3H6O3)\n- Formula: C3H6O3\n- SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)\n- MW: 90.08\n- Role: Intermediate \u2014 oxidized to pyruvate under cyanosulfidic conditions\n\n### Other Key Molecules\n\n**Hydroxylamine** (H3NO)\n- Formula: H3NO\n- SMILES: NO\n- InChI: InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\n- MW: 33.03\n- Role: Nitrogen donor for reductive amination of pyruvate to alanine\n\n**2-Aminopropanenitrile** (C3H6N2)\n- Formula: C3H6N2\n- SMILES: CC(C#N)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3\n- MW: 70.09\n- Role: Aminonitrile intermediate \u2014 Strecker-type precursor that hydrolyzes directly to alanine\n\n**L-Serine** (C3H7NO3)\n- Formula: C3H7NO3\n- SMILES: C(C(C(=O)O)N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)\n- MW: 105.09\n- Role: Amino acid precursor \u2014 reduced to alanine under hydrothermal conditions\n\n### Starting Molecules\n- **H2O**: SMILES: O, InChI: InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\n- **CO2**: SMILES: C(=O)=O, InChI: InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\n- **H2** (molecular hydrogen): SMILES: [HH], InChI: InChI=1S/H2/h1H\n- **NH3** (ammonia): SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\n\n## 3. Reaction Mechanisms and Conditions\n\n### Reactions Producing Pyruvate (Upstream)\n\n**Reaction U1: Oxaloacetate decarboxylation (iron-catalyzed)**\n- Oxaloacetate \u2192 Pyruvate + CO2\n- SMILES: O=C(O)CC(=O)C(=O)O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].O=C=O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: Ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n- Source: Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n- Mechanism: \u03b2-decarboxylation of oxaloacetate catalyzed by Fe\u00b2\u207a ions. This is thermodynamically favorable and proceeds readily at mild temperatures. Iron(II) acts as a Lewis acid to coordinate the \u03b2-keto acid and facilitate CO2 loss.\n\n**Reaction U2: Oxaloacetate decarboxylation (cyanosulfidic)**\n- Oxaloacetate \u2192 Pyruvate\n- SMILES: C(C(=O)C(=O)[O-])C(=O)[O-]>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-]\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Time: 1 hour\n- Source: Ritson (2021), *Science Advances*, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.abh3981\n- Mechanism: Decarboxylation under cyanosulfidic conditions as part of a prebiotic analog of the Krebs cycle.\n\n**Reaction U3: Lactic acid oxidation (cyanosulfidic)**\n- Lactic acid \u2192 Pyruvate\n- SMILES: CC(C(=O)[O-])O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-]\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Ritson (2021), *Science Advances*, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.abh3981\n- Mechanism: Oxidation of the \u03b1-hydroxy group of lactic acid to a ketone, converting lactate to pyruvate. This occurs under cyanosulfidic conditions where sulfur species act as oxidants.\n\n**Reaction U4: CO2 + H2 fixation to pyruvate (Fischer-Tropsch-type)**\n- 3 CO2 + 5 H2 \u2192 Pyruvate + 3 H2O\n- SMILES: [HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].O=C=O.O=C=O.O=C=O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 100\u00b0C (373 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: Elemental Iron (Fe\u2070), awaruite (Ni3Fe), magnetite (Fe3O4)\n- Source: Preiner, Igarashi, Muchowska, Yu, Varma, Kleinermanns, Nobu, Kamagata, T\u00fcys\u00fcz, Moran, and Martin (2020), *Nature Ecology & Evolution*, DOI: 10.1038/s41559-020-1125-6\n\n### Reactions Producing L-Alanine (Final Steps)\n\n**Reaction F1: Nitrile hydrolysis**\n- 2-aminopropanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Alanine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n\n**Reaction F2: Reductive amination (variant A)**\n- Pyruvate + hydroxylamine + H2O \u2192 L-Alanine\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO.O>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal, 70\u00b0C, Fe\u2070 + Fe\u00b2\u207a\n- Source: Muchowska et al. (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n\n**Reaction F3: Reductive amination (variant B)**\n- Pyruvate + hydroxylamine \u2192 L-Alanine + 2 H2O\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO>>CC(N)C(=O)O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal, 70\u00b0C, Fe\u2070 + Fe\u00b2\u207a\n- Source: Muchowska et al. (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n\n**Reaction F4: Serine reduction**\n- L-Serine + H2 \u2192 L-Alanine\n- SMILES: N[C@H](CO)C(=O)O.[HH]>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Source: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n\n## 4. Mineral Catalysts and Surfaces\n\nThe following mineral catalysts and metal species are experimentally documented for L-alanine synthesis reactions:\n\n- **Elemental Iron (Fe\u2070)**: Highly reactive, insoluble metallic iron. Acts as a strong reductant for CO2 fixation and drives reductive amination of pyruvate. Used in: pyruvate-to-alanine reductive amination (Muchowska et al., 2019) and CO2-to-pyruvate fixation (Preiner et al., 2020).\n\n- **Ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a)**: Soluble iron(II) species, abundant in anoxic early Earth oceans. Converts to insoluble Fe\u00b3\u207a in the presence of oxygen (not relevant on anoxic early Earth). Acts as Lewis acid catalyst for decarboxylation reactions and co-catalyst for reductive amination. Used in: oxaloacetate decarboxylation (Muchowska et al., 2019) and pyruvate-to-alanine reductive amination (Muchowska et al., 2019).\n\n- **Awaruite (Ni3Fe)**: A nickel-iron alloy mineral found in serpentinized ultramafic rocks. Provides catalytic surface for H2 activation and CO2 reduction. Used in: CO2-to-pyruvate Fischer-Tropsch-type synthesis (Preiner et al., 2020).\n\n- **Magnetite (Fe3O4)**: A mixed-valence iron oxide mineral common in hydrothermal systems. Facilitates CO2 adsorption and stepwise reduction. Used in: CO2-to-pyruvate synthesis (Preiner et al., 2020).\n\n## 5. Experimental Evidence\n\n### Study 1: Patel et al. (2015)\n- **Title**: \"Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Chemistry\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a unified cyanosulfidic chemistry starting from HCN and H2S can produce precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids. For alanine specifically, 2-aminopropanenitrile is formed via cyanosulfidic Strecker-like chemistry and hydrolyzes to alanine in aqueous conditions.\n\n### Study 2: Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019)\n- **Title**: \"Synthesis and breakdown of universal metabolic precursors promoted by iron\"\n- **Journal**: Nature\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that metallic iron (Fe\u2070) and ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a) at 70\u00b0C in water promote an interconnected network of reactions analogous to the core of biological metabolism. This includes: (a) interconversion of \u03b1-keto acids and \u03b1-amino acids via reductive amination with hydroxylamine, specifically pyruvate \u2192 alanine; (b) decarboxylation of oxaloacetate to pyruvate. This establishes a simple geochemical analog of the reductive TCA cycle and amino acid biosynthesis.\n\n### Study 3: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008)\n- **Title**: \"The Role of Submarine Hydrothermal Systems in the Synthesis of Amino Acids\"\n- **Journal**: Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres\n- **DOI**: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n- **Key finding**: Investigated amino acid synthesis under simulated submarine hydrothermal vent conditions. Demonstrated that L-serine can be reduced to L-alanine by molecular hydrogen, showing that hydrothermal processing can interconvert amino acids and expand the prebiotic amino acid inventory.\n\n### Study 4: Preiner et al. (2020)\n- **Title**: \"A hydrogen-dependent geochemical analogue of primordial carbon and energy metabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Ecology & Evolution\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/s41559-020-1125-6\n- **Key finding**: Showed that CO2 and H2 \u2014 the two simplest geochemical feedstocks available at serpentinizing hydrothermal vents \u2014 react on native iron, awaruite (Ni3Fe), and magnetite (Fe3O4) at 100\u00b0C to produce pyruvate and other metabolic intermediates. This provides a purely geochemical route from CO2 to the central metabolic hub (pyruvate) from which amino acids including alanine can be formed.\n\n### Study 5: Ritson (2021)\n- **Title**: \"A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle\"\n- **Journal**: Science Advances\n- **DOI**: 10.1126/sciadv.abh3981\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a cyanosulfidic reaction network can produce the intermediates of the Krebs (TCA) cycle abiotically. Relevant to alanine synthesis: (a) oxaloacetate undergoes decarboxylation to pyruvate under cyanosulfidic conditions; (b) lactic acid is oxidized to pyruvate. Both reactions provide additional prebiotic routes to the pyruvate hub molecule.\n\n## 6. Environmental Context\n\n### Hydrothermal Environment (Primary)\nThe majority of experimentally demonstrated alanine synthesis routes operate under hydrothermal conditions:\n- **Iron-catalyzed reductive amination** (Muchowska et al., 2019): Pyruvate + hydroxylamine \u2192 alanine at 70\u00b0C with Fe\u2070/Fe\u00b2\u207a. This mimics conditions at alkaline hydrothermal vents where metallic iron and dissolved Fe\u00b2\u207a are available from serpentinization, and temperatures of 70\u00b0C are found in the mixing zones between hot vent fluid and cooler ocean water.\n- **CO2 fixation to pyruvate** (Preiner et al., 2020): CO2 + H2 \u2192 pyruvate at 100\u00b0C on iron/awaruite/magnetite. Serpentinization of ultramafic rock produces abundant H2 and exposes native iron and nickel-iron alloys, making these minerals and conditions naturally co-located.\n- **Serine reduction** (Aubrey et al., 2008): L-serine + H2 \u2192 alanine under submarine hydrothermal conditions, where H2 from serpentinization provides the reductant.\n- **Oxaloacetate decarboxylation** (Muchowska et al., 2019): Fe\u00b2\u207a-catalyzed at 70\u00b0C.\n\n### Surface Environment (Secondary)\n- **Cyanosulfidic nitrile hydrolysis** (Patel et al., 2015): 2-aminopropanenitrile + H2O \u2192 alanine. This operates in surface pools where HCN-derived chemistry (UV-driven, evaporitic) produces aminonitrile intermediates that hydrolyze in wet phases.\n- **Cyanosulfidic Krebs cycle analogs** (Ritson, 2021): Lactic acid \u2192 pyruvate and oxaloacetate \u2192 pyruvate under surface cyanosulfidic conditions, feeding the pyruvate pool that is then available for transamination in environments where iron is present.\n\n### Environmental Interconnection\nThe synthesis network exhibits clear cross-environment flow:\n1. **Hydrothermal \u2192 Surface**: Pyruvate and other metabolic intermediates generated at hydrothermal vents could be transported to surface environments via ocean circulation.\n2. **Surface \u2192 Hydrothermal**: Cyanosulfidic products (aminonitriles, lactic acid, oxaloacetate) generated in surface pools could be washed into hydrothermal zones.\n3. **Both \u2192 Biochemical**: Alanine produced by any pathway enters the prebiotic amino acid pool available for peptide bond formation and proto-metabolic assembly.\n\n## 7. Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **Chirality**: All demonstrated prebiotic routes produce racemic alanine (equal L and D). The origin of homochirality (biological preference for L-amino acids) remains unresolved. Mineral surface adsorption or photolysis with circularly polarized light have been proposed.\n\n2. **Hydroxylamine availability**: The Muchowska et al. pathway requires hydroxylamine (NH2OH), whose prebiotic abundance is debated. It could form from NO reduction by Fe\u2070 or from NH3 oxidation, but quantitative constraints are needed.\n\n3. **Concentration problem**: Hydrothermal vent products are diluted in the ocean. Mechanisms for concentrating pyruvate and other intermediates (evaporitic pools, mineral adsorption, thermophoresis) are important for downstream reactions.\n\n4. **2-Aminopropanenitrile stability**: The Patel et al. pathway requires this intermediate to persist long enough for hydrolysis. Its stability under UV irradiation and varying pH conditions needs further study.\n\n5. **Pyruvate stability**: Pyruvate is thermally labile and can decarboxylate to acetaldehyde + CO2 at elevated temperatures. The balance between pyruvate production and decomposition at hydrothermal temperatures is a key kinetic question.\n\n=== END CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.74\n- Feedback: The complete network is connected, has sufficient molecule and reaction nodes, and contains credible routes to alanine within its declared feedstock boundary. The deterministic check establishes that all ten reactions conserve atoms and charge with appropriate coefficients; none is a conservation violation. The coefficients associated with S8 and separate L/D products are reasonable. Correct the mol_017 serialization to represent one Fe atom with formal charge +2 consistently; this metadata discrepancy does not override the reported reaction balances. Explicit consumption of metallic iron and sulfur, correction of the hydroxylamine redox equation, and representation of racemic products are strengths. L-alanine is legitimately present in the predicted racemate, so lack of enantiomeric enrichment is not a target failure. P2 and P9 provide the strongest supported chemistry, but the complete network is not established by those branches alone: rxn_004 and rxn_010 remain speculative, rxn_003 needs selective-product evidence, and rxn_006 needs demonstrated acetaldehyde production and capture. Reaction records generally lack quantitative conditions and reaction-specific citations. Literature alignment is strongest for oxaloacetate decarboxylation, iron-driven amination and Strecker chemistry; the review does not establish every proposed implementation. Environmental integration requires explicit catalyst availability, pH staging, intermediate retention and management of potentially incompatible carryover. Shared steps and ten graph routes are not independent experimental confirmations. The network is therefore not approved at the required overall confidence threshold of 0.9.\n- Issues: ['Preiner et al. (2020) supports mineral-mediated CO2/H2 conversion to metabolic intermediates, and Muchowska et al. (2019) supports the downstream iron-driven amination. However, rxn_001 does not specify the catalytic mineral, gas pressure, pH or residence time needed for pyruvate production. The route is chemically credible but incompletely specified; its alanine product appropriately includes both enantiomers.', 'Serine conversion to pyruvate and ammonia is a plausible net transformation, potentially involving dehydration followed by imine hydrolysis. Nevertheless, the supplied literature does not establish selective pyruvate production under the unspecified thermal conditions of rxn_003. Competing serine decomposition and subsequent pyruvate degradation could limit the otherwise credible amination branch.', \"The sulfur-coupled oxidation conserves atoms and has explicit redox bookkeeping, but balance does not establish an effective photochemical reaction. The review's attribution of lactate oxidation to cyanosulfidic chemistry does not specifically validate S8 as the operative oxidant under this proposed system. Transfer into the iron-driven branch also requires evaluating sulfide carryover and iron precipitation or passivation.\", 'The downstream Strecker sequence is chemically established, but this complete route depends on inadequately specified CO2 fixation and preparative acetaldehyde production. The review identifies pyruvate decarboxylation as a possible degradation process, not a demonstrated acetaldehyde capture protocol. Volatile-product retention and sufficiently effective nitrile and amide hydrolysis remain unresolved.', 'Oxaloacetate provides a credible pyruvate source, and the carbon flow through acetaldehyde and the Strecker intermediate is coherent. The principal uncertainty is whether rxn_006 supplies recoverable acetaldehyde selectively enough to support the downstream sequence. The surface hydrolysis steps also need explicit conditions rather than an environmental label alone.', 'The route is stoichiometrically coherent, but selective serine-to-pyruvate conversion and subsequent acetaldehyde recovery are both insufficiently established. Ammonia generated during serine processing could contribute to the Strecker feed, although its retention and concentration are not demonstrated. Established downstream chemistry does not remove these upstream uncertainties.', 'This route combines an unvalidated S8-dependent photooxidation with uncertain thermal acetaldehyde production and capture. Its connectivity and atom balance are satisfactory, but productive material flow has not been established. The environmental concerns arise from unspecified irradiation, sulfur speciation, transport and volatile retention, not from the number or ordering of environments.', \"The net hydrogenolysis is balanced, but selective replacement of serine's hydroxyl group by hydrogen is not established merely by supplying H2 under hydrothermal conditions. A catalyst capable of H2 activation and selective C-O cleavage, together with operating conditions and product evidence, is missing. The review's attribution to Aubrey et al. (2008) should be checked against direct experimental evidence for this specific transformation.\"]\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.84\n- Feedback: The complete graph is connected, has sufficient molecule and reaction nodes, and uses molecule IDs consistently. All 12 reactions pass the supplied ground-truth atom/charge check with chemically reasonable coefficients; none is a conservation violation. Sacrificial iron consumption and separate L/D alanine products are represented appropriately. L-alanine in a racemate satisfies the declared objective, and the evaluation accepts the stated external-feedstock boundary without requiring feedstock self-sufficiency. The strongest support is for oxaloacetate decarboxylation, iron/hydroxylamine conversion of pyruvate, and conventional Strecker chemistry. Alignment with Muchowska, Preiner and Patel is substantial, but reaction-specific citations and protocols are absent from the reaction records. In particular, the numerical operating windows mentioned in the preamble are not actually supplied. Mineral-specific CO2-fixation performance, productive cyanohydrin equilibration, environmentally realistic nitrile and hydantoin hydrolysis, and intermediate survival during transfers remain insufficiently constrained. Explicitly labeling extensions as conditional is appropriate, but does not establish their performance. The ten graph routes are not treated as independent experimental confirmations, and shared molecules, convergence counts and environment ordering do not influence the score. Credible routes to the target exist, but the incomplete condition specification and conditional branches keep confidence below the 0.90 threshold; the complete network is therefore not approved.\n- Issues: ['The hydantoin branch is recognizable Bucherer-Bergs-related chemistry, and both proposed net transformations are balanced. However, the supplied studies do not establish alaninonitrile capture as 5-methylhydantoin under the proposed surface conditions or complete release of alanine under environmentally realistic alkaline hydrothermal conditions. Ring opening can leave an N-carbamoyl amino acid, so ring opening alone does not demonstrate target formation.', 'All steps are conservation-consistent, and the proposed sequence has a plausible chemical rationale. Confidence is limited by the combination of unverified lactonitrile-to-alaninonitrile performance, conditional hydantoin formation and demanding complete hydantoin hydrolysis. Hydantoin storage could be useful, but protection during transport and subsequent alanine release are hypotheses here, not demonstrated benefits.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Alanine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct ten distinct reaction-node sequences using three pyruvate-supply branches, three chemically different reductive-amination branches, and one acetaldehyde Strecker branch. The two oxaloacetate branches are catalyst-dependent alternatives, not different carbon-fixation mechanisms. All alanine-forming reactions produce both L- and D-alanine; L-alanine is present, but enantiomeric enrichment is not claimed. The supplied review's two hydroxylamine equations are treated as one transformation, with sacrificial iron consumption and electron balance made explicit. Unsupported serine hydrogenolysis, sulfur-driven lactate photooxidation, acetaldehyde capture from pyruvate, and hydantoin chemistry are excluded. Additional ammonia-based aminations draw on Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003); their integration is explicitly conditional. These are ten graph routes, not ten independently demonstrated end-to-end syntheses. Numerical conditions identified as proposed are experimental screening specifications, not claimed published protocols or guaranteed productive operating windows. External feedstocks include oxaloacetate, hydroxylamine, acetaldehyde and HCN; their formation is outside this network boundary. Neutral parent formulas represent acid-base pools except where charged species are required for redox accounting. Stereo-unspecified aminonitrile and amide nodes represent racemic precursor pools. The single environment_formed value records a representative formation environment where more than one applies.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"proton\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"native iron\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe+2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(2+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferrous ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H4O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxobutanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"oxaloacetic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeH2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) hydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferrous hydroxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeH3O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(III) hydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferric hydroxide, formal hydrated Fe(III) sink\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) sulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"mackinawite\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeS2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) disulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyrite\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alaninonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alaninamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H8N2O/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H2,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Awaruite-mediated hydrogen-dependent CO2 fixation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a hydrogen-activating mineral catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed: 25 bar total initial gas pressure, H2:CO2 = 4:1\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: 1-10 g/L freshly characterized awaruite, 16-72 h, initial room-temperature pH 9-10 before gas charging. Measure pH after CO2 uptake rather than assuming it remains alkaline. Use an anoxic, gas-retaining reactor. Quantify pyruvate against blanks and distinguish it from more abundant C1/C2 products. Filter mineral particles and cool promptly before downstream processing.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A lumped, multistep surface reaction combines hydrogen activation, CO2 reduction and carbon-carbon bond formation. The detailed sequence to pyruvate is not established and is not presented as a proven Fischer-Tropsch mechanism.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) supports hydrogen-dependent mineral-mediated formation of metabolic intermediates, including pyruvate, near 373 K. Only one mineral implementation is included; the review's mineral combinations are not multiplied into presumed equivalent pyruvate-producing routes. The numerical loading, gas composition and residence-time window above are proposed rather than extracted experimental conditions. Mineral-specific pyruvate yield, catalyst history and reproducibility remain essential checks. Pyruvate is a minor product, so neither preparative selectivity nor sufficient concentration for subsequent chemistry is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Ferrous-ion-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"dissolved iron(II), acting as a Lewis-acid promoter\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-353 K proposed; cited experimental benchmark is 343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed: 10 bar in a closed aqueous mixing-zone analogue\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"aqueous iron(II)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: 0.1-5 mM oxaloacetate, 0.1-5 mM dissolved Fe(II), initial pH 5-7, 1-24 h with time-resolved sampling. Exclude oxygen and initially exclude sulfide, which would remove dissolved Fe(II). Avoid prolonged storage of the resulting pyruvate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Iron(II) coordinates the beta-keto-acid functionality, facilitates carbon-carbon bond cleavage and CO2 loss, and permits the resulting enol to tautomerize to pyruvate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) supports iron-promoted oxaloacetate-to-pyruvate conversion at 343 K. The slightly warmer proposed window respects the requested hydrothermal range but is an extrapolation requiring kinetic verification. Oxaloacetate is an explicitly supplied precursor, not an internally generated carbon-fixation product. Sulfide precipitation, metal complexation and pyruvate decomposition must be controlled before transferring this stream to an amination stage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Uncatalyzed aqueous oxaloacetate decarboxylation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Proposed: 303-323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: 0.1-5 mM oxaloacetate, initial pH 4-6, 1-48 h. Use a shaded or sediment-covered aqueous pool analogue, with no intentionally added transition-metal catalyst. Follow oxaloacetate disappearance and pyruvate appearance separately. Transfer locally to a geothermal reaction zone only if pyruvate retention is demonstrated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The intrinsically labile beta-keto acid loses CO2 through an enol-forming decarboxylation, followed by tautomerization to pyruvate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson (2021), as cited in the supplied review, supports a surface oxaloacetate-to-pyruvate connection. Ordinary beta-keto-acid chemistry also supports spontaneous decarboxylation without requiring a sulfur oxidant or UV excitation. The specific metal-free pH and residence-time window proposed here is not established by that citation and is a conditional implementation. This is a catalyst-free alternative to rxn_002, not an additional carbon-skeleton synthesis mechanism.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-driven hydroxylamine conversion of pyruvate to racemic alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"native iron, explicitly consumed as a stoichiometric reductant\",\n        \"dissolved iron(II), as a coordination promoter\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-353 K proposed; cited experimental benchmark is 343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed: 10 bar in a closed aqueous reactor\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"iron(II) coordination promoter; iron metal is not treated as a regenerated catalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: 0.01-1 mM pyruvate, 10-100 mM total hydroxylamine, 0.05-0.5 mol/L iron-metal inventory, initial pH 5-7, 4-48 h. Maintain anoxia and exclude sulfide carryover that could passivate iron. The explicit proton node supplies formal redox bookkeeping; actual proton activity is buffered and evolves during iron corrosion. Assay hydroxylamine loss, dissolved iron and both alanine enantiomers.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine condenses with pyruvate to an oxime-type intermediate. Reduction of the carbon-nitrogen functionality and cleavage of the nitrogen-oxygen bond give alanine. Formation of alanine from pyruvate and hydroxylamine requires four reducing equivalents per alanine molecule.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) supports iron-driven amino-acid formation from keto-acid precursors with hydroxylamine. Metallic iron oxidation to Fe(II) is included explicitly, correcting the redox-incomplete equations in the supplied review. Its two water-accounting variants are not counted as different pathways. The proposed temperature, concentrations and pressure are not a reproduced published protocol. Hydroxylamine availability, competing reduction of hydroxylamine to ammonia, and trace-level pyruvate capture are major limitations. No asymmetric catalyst is present, so both alanine enantiomers are outputs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Ferrous-hydroxide-driven reductive amination with ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"iron(II) hydroxide, consumed as a reductant rather than assumed to be catalytic\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed: 10 bar, closed anoxic aqueous system\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: 0.01-1 mM pyruvate, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia/ammonium, 0.05-0.2 mol/L freshly precipitated Fe(OH)2 inventory, initial pH 8-9.5, 24-168 h. Retain ammonia in the closed system. Remove sulfide before this branch because it changes the iron mineral assemblage. Measure iron oxidation states and solid phases rather than inferring them from alanine detection.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine or iminium intermediate with pyruvate. Oxidation of ferrous iron supplies the two electrons required for reduction to alanine. Hydrated ferric hydroxide represents the formal oxidized-iron sink in the atom and electron ledger.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative at the integrated-network level relative to the supplied review, but the reaction class has additional experimental support in Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003), 'Primordial reductive amination revisited', which investigated ferrous-hydroxide-supported reductive amination. It is included because ammonia is a chemically distinct nitrogen source that avoids reliance on hydroxylamine. The proposed loadings, performance at dilute pyruvate concentrations and natural co-location are not demonstrated here. Fe(OH)3 is a formal hydrated Fe(III) product, not a claim that a particular crystalline hydroxide was measured; oxyhydroxide or mixed-valence phases could require revised phase-specific bookkeeping.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-sulfide and hydrogen-sulfide-supported reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"mackinawite, explicitly included as a consumed iron-sulfide phase\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed: 10 bar in a closed, sulfide-retaining aqueous reactor\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: 0.01-1 mM pyruvate, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia/ammonium, 0.05-0.2 mol/L FeS inventory, 0.01-0.1 M total dissolved sulfide, initial pH 6.5-8.5, 24-168 h. Establish this as a separate sulfide-rich reactor rather than adding sulfide to the hydroxylamine/iron-metal branch. Quantify pyrite formation, competing sulfur phases and alanine production.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia-derived imine formation is followed by reduction coupled to conversion of monosulfide and dissolved sulfide into disulfide in pyrite. Sulfur oxidation supplies reducing equivalents without molecular oxygen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative at the integrated-network level relative to the supplied review, with additional reaction-class support from Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) for iron-sulfide-associated reductive amination. This branch tests an ammonia-based alternative in a geochemically different reducing assemblage. The explicit FeS/H2S-to-FeS2 ledger avoids treating mineral reducing power as unlimited catalysis. However, the proposed system must demonstrate coupled pyrite and alanine formation: pyrite-forming thermodynamics alone does not establish reaction rate, selectivity or the identity of the operative reductant. Surface passivation and competing sulfide chemistry are key limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde Strecker formation of alaninonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_018\",\n        \"mol_019\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_020\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Proposed: 300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in a gas-retaining pore or closed laboratory analogue\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: 10-100 mM acetaldehyde, 10-100 mM total HCN/cyanide, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia/ammonium, initial pH 8-9, 1-7 days. Use covered or sediment-shielded water rather than assuming aminonitrile survival under continuous strong UV. Measure acetaldehyde and HCN losses as well as aminonitrile formation. Feedstock concentration and volatile retention are prerequisites, not assumed consequences of wet-dry cycling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetaldehyde and ammonia form an imine/iminium pool; cyanide addition gives racemic 2-aminopropanenitrile. Water is the net condensation coproduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Classical Strecker chemistry supports this carbon-nitrogen bond-forming sequence (Strecker, 1850), while Patel et al. (2015) supports aminonitrile precursors within prebiotic cyanosulfidic chemistry. The proposed environmental concentrations and residence time are not attributed to either paper as measured protocols. Acetaldehyde is supplied directly, avoiding an unverified pyruvate-decarboxylation and volatile-capture step. Competing cyanohydrin formation may occur, but a separate productive cyanohydrin pathway is not asserted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydration of alaninonitrile to alaninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_020\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_021\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"aqueous acid, providing regenerated proton-transfer catalysis\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Proposed: 393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed: 10 bar, sufficient to retain liquid water over this temperature range\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"aqueous acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: 1-50 mM aminonitrile, initial room-temperature pH 1-2, 24-168 h with intermediate sampling. Use an acidic, locally connected subsurface geothermal compartment; do not assume transport through the open ocean. Remove or quantify residual cyanide and ammonia before acidification. The amide may be transient and need not be isolated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Protonation activates the nitrile toward water addition. Proton transfers and tautomerization convert the hydrated nitrile into alaninamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) supports the aminonitrile-to-amino-acid relationship, and sequential nitrile hydration followed by amide hydrolysis is established Strecker chemistry. The supplied review does not establish rapid neutral hydrolysis at ambient temperature, so that claim is not adopted. The hotter acidic implementation is a proposed geochemical extrapolation rather than a verified protocol from Patel et al. The productive hydration rate must be measured against decomposition, and mineral neutralization could prevent the required acidity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Alanine release by complete alaninamide hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_021\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"aqueous acid, providing regenerated proton-transfer catalysis\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Proposed: 393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed: 10 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"aqueous acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continue the acidic hydrolysis stage for a proposed total residence time of 24-336 h, initial room-temperature pH 1-2, with time-resolved analysis. Quantify residual amide and free alanine independently; amide formation alone is not target formation. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions but is represented by its neutral parent formula. Cool promptly after productive hydrolysis to limit further decomposition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate cleaves the carbon-nitrogen bond, releasing the carboxylic-acid amino-acid pool and ammonia/ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Complete aminonitrile hydrolysis is the alanine-producing endpoint associated with the precursor chemistry of Patel et al. (2015) and classical Strecker synthesis. This node explicitly requires amide cleavage rather than treating hydration as completed amino-acid synthesis. The proposed geothermal residence time is not a demonstrated prebiotic yield claim. Full conversion, alanine survival and maintenance of acidity are the branch's principal kinetic and environmental bottlenecks. Because the precursor pool is racemic, both L- and D-alanine are produced.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation followed by hydroxylamine/iron amination\",\n      \"description\": \"CO2 and H2 supply pyruvate on awaruite, followed by sacrificial-iron reduction with externally supplied hydroxylamine. Supported reaction classes are connected conditionally; dilute-product capture and compatible staging remain unproven.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by hydroxylamine amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied oxaloacetate forms pyruvate with Fe(II), followed by iron-driven hydroxylamine conversion. This has the closest alignment with the iron-mediated chemistry emphasized in the supplied review.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by hydroxylamine amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Catalyst-free surface decarboxylation supplies pyruvate to a locally connected, warmer iron-containing compartment. Product retention and pH adjustment must be demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation followed by ferrous-hydroxide/ammonia amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Mineral-generated pyruvate enters an ammonia-based reductive-amination stage. This avoids hydroxylamine but depends on the conditional ferrous-hydroxide implementation and verification of its oxidized mineral products.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by ferrous-hydroxide amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Oxaloacetate-derived pyruvate is transferred to a more alkaline ammonia/ferrous-hydroxide assemblage. Iron precipitation and ammonia retention require explicit staging.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by ferrous-hydroxide amination\",\n      \"description\": \"An uncatalyzed pyruvate supply branch feeds the conditional ammonia/ferrous-hydroxide reaction after local heating and alkalinity adjustment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation followed by sulfide-associated ammonia amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate generated on awaruite is separated from the fixation mineral before entering a distinct FeS/H2S-rich ammonia reactor. Coupling of sulfur oxidation to alanine production requires verification.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by sulfide-associated amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Fe(II)-promoted decarboxylation supplies pyruvate before deliberate transition to a sulfide-rich mineral assemblage. This is a staged process, not an assumption that soluble Fe(II) and concentrated sulfide remain compatible.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by sulfide-associated amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Surface-generated pyruvate feeds a locally connected sulfide-bearing hydrothermal compartment. This combines a catalyst-free supply variant with the conditional FeS/H2S ammonia-reduction branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde Strecker synthesis followed by complete staged hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde, ammonia and HCN form racemic alaninonitrile in a protected surface solution. Acidic geothermal hydration forms alaninamide, and subsequent complete amide hydrolysis releases racemic alanine, including the L target. Volatile retention, acidity maintenance and measured completion of both hydrolysis stages are required.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_020\",\n        \"mol_021\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_012\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "Construct ten distinct reaction-node sequences using three pyruvate-supply branches, three chemically different reductive-amination branches, and one acetaldehyde Strecker branch. The two oxaloacetate branches are catalyst-dependent alternatives, not different carbon-fixation mechanisms. All alanine-forming reactions produce both L- and D-alanine; L-alanine is present, but enantiomeric enrichment is not claimed. The supplied review's two hydroxylamine equations are treated as one transformation, with sacrificial iron consumption and electron balance made explicit. Unsupported serine hydrogenolysis, sulfur-driven lactate photooxidation, acetaldehyde capture from pyruvate, and hydantoin chemistry are excluded. Additional ammonia-based aminations draw on Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003); their integration is explicitly conditional. These are ten graph routes, not ten independently demonstrated end-to-end syntheses. Numerical conditions identified as proposed are experimental screening specifications, not claimed published protocols or guaranteed productive operating windows. External feedstocks include oxaloacetate, hydroxylamine, acetaldehyde and HCN; their formation is outside this network boundary. Neutral parent formulas represent acid-base pools except where charged species are required for redox accounting. Stereo-unspecified aminonitrile and amide nodes represent racemic precursor pools. The single environment_formed value records a representative formation environment where more than one applies.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydron", "common_name": "proton", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "native iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C4H4O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxobutanedioic acid", "common_name": "oxaloacetic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "D-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "FeH2O2", "iupac_name": "iron(II) hydroxide", "common_name": "ferrous hydroxide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "FeH3O3", "iupac_name": "iron(III) hydroxide", "common_name": "ferric hydroxide, formal hydrated Fe(III) sink", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "FeS", "iupac_name": "iron(II) sulfide", "common_name": "mackinawite", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "FeS2", "iupac_name": "iron(II) disulfide", "common_name": "pyrite", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "alaninonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C3H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanamide", "common_name": "alaninamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H8N2O/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H2,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Awaruite-mediated hydrogen-dependent CO2 fixation to pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a hydrogen-activating mineral catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373 K", "pressure": "Proposed: 25 bar total initial gas pressure, H2:CO2 = 4:1", "catalyst": "awaruite", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: 1-10 g/L freshly characterized awaruite, 16-72 h, initial room-temperature pH 9-10 before gas charging. Measure pH after CO2 uptake rather than assuming it remains alkaline. Use an anoxic, gas-retaining reactor. Quantify pyruvate against blanks and distinguish it from more abundant C1/C2 products. Filter mineral particles and cool promptly before downstream processing."}, "mechanism": "A lumped, multistep surface reaction combines hydrogen activation, CO2 reduction and carbon-carbon bond formation. The detailed sequence to pyruvate is not established and is not presented as a proven Fischer-Tropsch mechanism.", "reasoning": "Preiner et al. (2020) supports hydrogen-dependent mineral-mediated formation of metabolic intermediates, including pyruvate, near 373 K. Only one mineral implementation is included; the review's mineral combinations are not multiplied into presumed equivalent pyruvate-producing routes. The numerical loading, gas composition and residence-time window above are proposed rather than extracted experimental conditions. Mineral-specific pyruvate yield, catalyst history and reproducibility remain essential checks. Pyruvate is a minor product, so neither preparative selectivity nor sufficient concentration for subsequent chemistry is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Ferrous-ion-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation", "inputs": ["mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["dissolved iron(II), acting as a Lewis-acid promoter"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-353 K proposed; cited experimental benchmark is 343 K", "pressure": "Proposed: 10 bar in a closed aqueous mixing-zone analogue", "catalyst": "aqueous iron(II)", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: 0.1-5 mM oxaloacetate, 0.1-5 mM dissolved Fe(II), initial pH 5-7, 1-24 h with time-resolved sampling. Exclude oxygen and initially exclude sulfide, which would remove dissolved Fe(II). Avoid prolonged storage of the resulting pyruvate."}, "mechanism": "Iron(II) coordinates the beta-keto-acid functionality, facilitates carbon-carbon bond cleavage and CO2 loss, and permits the resulting enol to tautomerize to pyruvate.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) supports iron-promoted oxaloacetate-to-pyruvate conversion at 343 K. The slightly warmer proposed window respects the requested hydrothermal range but is an extrapolation requiring kinetic verification. Oxaloacetate is an explicitly supplied precursor, not an internally generated carbon-fixation product. Sulfide precipitation, metal complexation and pyruvate decomposition must be controlled before transferring this stream to an amination stage."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Uncatalyzed aqueous oxaloacetate decarboxylation", "inputs": ["mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Proposed: 303-323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: 0.1-5 mM oxaloacetate, initial pH 4-6, 1-48 h. Use a shaded or sediment-covered aqueous pool analogue, with no intentionally added transition-metal catalyst. Follow oxaloacetate disappearance and pyruvate appearance separately. Transfer locally to a geothermal reaction zone only if pyruvate retention is demonstrated."}, "mechanism": "The intrinsically labile beta-keto acid loses CO2 through an enol-forming decarboxylation, followed by tautomerization to pyruvate.", "reasoning": "Ritson (2021), as cited in the supplied review, supports a surface oxaloacetate-to-pyruvate connection. Ordinary beta-keto-acid chemistry also supports spontaneous decarboxylation without requiring a sulfur oxidant or UV excitation. The specific metal-free pH and residence-time window proposed here is not established by that citation and is a conditional implementation. This is a catalyst-free alternative to rxn_002, not an additional carbon-skeleton synthesis mechanism."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Iron-driven hydroxylamine conversion of pyruvate to racemic alanine", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_006", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["native iron, explicitly consumed as a stoichiometric reductant", "dissolved iron(II), as a coordination promoter"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-353 K proposed; cited experimental benchmark is 343 K", "pressure": "Proposed: 10 bar in a closed aqueous reactor", "catalyst": "iron(II) coordination promoter; iron metal is not treated as a regenerated catalyst", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: 0.01-1 mM pyruvate, 10-100 mM total hydroxylamine, 0.05-0.5 mol/L iron-metal inventory, initial pH 5-7, 4-48 h. Maintain anoxia and exclude sulfide carryover that could passivate iron. The explicit proton node supplies formal redox bookkeeping; actual proton activity is buffered and evolves during iron corrosion. Assay hydroxylamine loss, dissolved iron and both alanine enantiomers."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine condenses with pyruvate to an oxime-type intermediate. Reduction of the carbon-nitrogen functionality and cleavage of the nitrogen-oxygen bond give alanine. Formation of alanine from pyruvate and hydroxylamine requires four reducing equivalents per alanine molecule.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) supports iron-driven amino-acid formation from keto-acid precursors with hydroxylamine. Metallic iron oxidation to Fe(II) is included explicitly, correcting the redox-incomplete equations in the supplied review. Its two water-accounting variants are not counted as different pathways. The proposed temperature, concentrations and pressure are not a reproduced published protocol. Hydroxylamine availability, competing reduction of hydroxylamine to ammonia, and trace-level pyruvate capture are major limitations. No asymmetric catalyst is present, so both alanine enantiomers are outputs."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Ferrous-hydroxide-driven reductive amination with ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_010", "mol_013", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_014"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["iron(II) hydroxide, consumed as a reductant rather than assumed to be catalytic"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373 K", "pressure": "Proposed: 10 bar, closed anoxic aqueous system", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: 0.01-1 mM pyruvate, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia/ammonium, 0.05-0.2 mol/L freshly precipitated Fe(OH)2 inventory, initial pH 8-9.5, 24-168 h. Retain ammonia in the closed system. Remove sulfide before this branch because it changes the iron mineral assemblage. Measure iron oxidation states and solid phases rather than inferring them from alanine detection."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine or iminium intermediate with pyruvate. Oxidation of ferrous iron supplies the two electrons required for reduction to alanine. Hydrated ferric hydroxide represents the formal oxidized-iron sink in the atom and electron ledger.", "reasoning": "Speculative at the integrated-network level relative to the supplied review, but the reaction class has additional experimental support in Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003), 'Primordial reductive amination revisited', which investigated ferrous-hydroxide-supported reductive amination. It is included because ammonia is a chemically distinct nitrogen source that avoids reliance on hydroxylamine. The proposed loadings, performance at dilute pyruvate concentrations and natural co-location are not demonstrated here. Fe(OH)3 is a formal hydrated Fe(III) product, not a claim that a particular crystalline hydroxide was measured; oxyhydroxide or mixed-valence phases could require revised phase-specific bookkeeping."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Iron-sulfide and hydrogen-sulfide-supported reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_010", "mol_015", "mol_016"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_017", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["mackinawite, explicitly included as a consumed iron-sulfide phase"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373 K", "pressure": "Proposed: 10 bar in a closed, sulfide-retaining aqueous reactor", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: 0.01-1 mM pyruvate, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia/ammonium, 0.05-0.2 mol/L FeS inventory, 0.01-0.1 M total dissolved sulfide, initial pH 6.5-8.5, 24-168 h. Establish this as a separate sulfide-rich reactor rather than adding sulfide to the hydroxylamine/iron-metal branch. Quantify pyrite formation, competing sulfur phases and alanine production."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia-derived imine formation is followed by reduction coupled to conversion of monosulfide and dissolved sulfide into disulfide in pyrite. Sulfur oxidation supplies reducing equivalents without molecular oxygen.", "reasoning": "Speculative at the integrated-network level relative to the supplied review, with additional reaction-class support from Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) for iron-sulfide-associated reductive amination. This branch tests an ammonia-based alternative in a geochemically different reducing assemblage. The explicit FeS/H2S-to-FeS2 ledger avoids treating mineral reducing power as unlimited catalysis. However, the proposed system must demonstrate coupled pyrite and alanine formation: pyrite-forming thermodynamics alone does not establish reaction rate, selectivity or the identity of the operative reductant. Surface passivation and competing sulfide chemistry are key limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Acetaldehyde Strecker formation of alaninonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_019", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Proposed: 300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in a gas-retaining pore or closed laboratory analogue", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: 10-100 mM acetaldehyde, 10-100 mM total HCN/cyanide, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia/ammonium, initial pH 8-9, 1-7 days. Use covered or sediment-shielded water rather than assuming aminonitrile survival under continuous strong UV. Measure acetaldehyde and HCN losses as well as aminonitrile formation. Feedstock concentration and volatile retention are prerequisites, not assumed consequences of wet-dry cycling."}, "mechanism": "Acetaldehyde and ammonia form an imine/iminium pool; cyanide addition gives racemic 2-aminopropanenitrile. Water is the net condensation coproduct.", "reasoning": "Classical Strecker chemistry supports this carbon-nitrogen bond-forming sequence (Strecker, 1850), while Patel et al. (2015) supports aminonitrile precursors within prebiotic cyanosulfidic chemistry. The proposed environmental concentrations and residence time are not attributed to either paper as measured protocols. Acetaldehyde is supplied directly, avoiding an unverified pyruvate-decarboxylation and volatile-capture step. Competing cyanohydrin formation may occur, but a separate productive cyanohydrin pathway is not asserted."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Acid-catalyzed hydration of alaninonitrile to alaninamide", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_021"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["aqueous acid, providing regenerated proton-transfer catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Proposed: 393-423 K", "pressure": "Proposed: 10 bar, sufficient to retain liquid water over this temperature range", "catalyst": "aqueous acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: 1-50 mM aminonitrile, initial room-temperature pH 1-2, 24-168 h with intermediate sampling. Use an acidic, locally connected subsurface geothermal compartment; do not assume transport through the open ocean. Remove or quantify residual cyanide and ammonia before acidification. The amide may be transient and need not be isolated."}, "mechanism": "Protonation activates the nitrile toward water addition. Proton transfers and tautomerization convert the hydrated nitrile into alaninamide.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) supports the aminonitrile-to-amino-acid relationship, and sequential nitrile hydration followed by amide hydrolysis is established Strecker chemistry. The supplied review does not establish rapid neutral hydrolysis at ambient temperature, so that claim is not adopted. The hotter acidic implementation is a proposed geochemical extrapolation rather than a verified protocol from Patel et al. The productive hydration rate must be measured against decomposition, and mineral neutralization could prevent the required acidity."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Alanine release by complete alaninamide hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["aqueous acid, providing regenerated proton-transfer catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Proposed: 393-423 K", "pressure": "Proposed: 10 bar", "catalyst": "aqueous acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Continue the acidic hydrolysis stage for a proposed total residence time of 24-336 h, initial room-temperature pH 1-2, with time-resolved analysis. Quantify residual amide and free alanine independently; amide formation alone is not target formation. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions but is represented by its neutral parent formula. Cool promptly after productive hydrolysis to limit further decomposition."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate cleaves the carbon-nitrogen bond, releasing the carboxylic-acid amino-acid pool and ammonia/ammonium.", "reasoning": "Complete aminonitrile hydrolysis is the alanine-producing endpoint associated with the precursor chemistry of Patel et al. (2015) and classical Strecker synthesis. This node explicitly requires amide cleavage rather than treating hydration as completed amino-acid synthesis. The proposed geothermal residence time is not a demonstrated prebiotic yield claim. Full conversion, alanine survival and maintenance of acidity are the branch's principal kinetic and environmental bottlenecks. Because the precursor pool is racemic, both L- and D-alanine are produced."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "CO2 fixation followed by hydroxylamine/iron amination", "description": "CO2 and H2 supply pyruvate on awaruite, followed by sacrificial-iron reduction with externally supplied hydroxylamine. Supported reaction classes are connected conditionally; dilute-product capture and compatible staging remain unproven.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Iron-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by hydroxylamine amination", "description": "Supplied oxaloacetate forms pyruvate with Fe(II), followed by iron-driven hydroxylamine conversion. This has the closest alignment with the iron-mediated chemistry emphasized in the supplied review.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by hydroxylamine amination", "description": "Catalyst-free surface decarboxylation supplies pyruvate to a locally connected, warmer iron-containing compartment. Product retention and pH adjustment must be demonstrated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "CO2 fixation followed by ferrous-hydroxide/ammonia amination", "description": "Mineral-generated pyruvate enters an ammonia-based reductive-amination stage. This avoids hydroxylamine but depends on the conditional ferrous-hydroxide implementation and verification of its oxidized mineral products.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Iron-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by ferrous-hydroxide amination", "description": "Oxaloacetate-derived pyruvate is transferred to a more alkaline ammonia/ferrous-hydroxide assemblage. Iron precipitation and ammonia retention require explicit staging.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by ferrous-hydroxide amination", "description": "An uncatalyzed pyruvate supply branch feeds the conditional ammonia/ferrous-hydroxide reaction after local heating and alkalinity adjustment.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "CO2 fixation followed by sulfide-associated ammonia amination", "description": "Pyruvate generated on awaruite is separated from the fixation mineral before entering a distinct FeS/H2S-rich ammonia reactor. Coupling of sulfur oxidation to alanine production requires verification.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Iron-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by sulfide-associated amination", "description": "Fe(II)-promoted decarboxylation supplies pyruvate before deliberate transition to a sulfide-rich mineral assemblage. This is a staged process, not an assumption that soluble Fe(II) and concentrated sulfide remain compatible.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by sulfide-associated amination", "description": "Surface-generated pyruvate feeds a locally connected sulfide-bearing hydrothermal compartment. This combines a catalyst-free supply variant with the conditional FeS/H2S ammonia-reduction branch.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Acetaldehyde Strecker synthesis followed by complete staged hydrolysis", "description": "Acetaldehyde, ammonia and HCN form racemic alaninonitrile in a protected surface solution. Acidic geothermal hydration forms alaninamide, and subsequent complete amide hydrolysis releases racemic alanine, including the L target. Volatile retention, acidity maintenance and measured completion of both hydrolysis stages are required.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_020", "mol_021"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_003", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_001", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_001"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_003", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_007", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": [], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_009"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_011", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_012", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 398102.91743278503, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T05:36:11.659721", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct ten distinct reaction-node sequences using three pyruvate-supply branches, three chemically different reductive-amination branches, and one acetaldehyde Strecker branch. The two oxaloacetate branches are catalyst-dependent alternatives, not different carbon-fixation mechanisms. All alanine-forming reactions produce both L- and D-alanine; L-alanine is present, but enantiomeric enrichment is not claimed. The supplied review's two hydroxylamine equations are treated as one transformation, with sacrificial iron consumption and electron balance made explicit. Unsupported serine hydrogenolysis, sulfur-driven lactate photooxidation, acetaldehyde capture from pyruvate, and hydantoin chemistry are excluded. Additional ammonia-based aminations draw on Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003); their integration is explicitly conditional. These are ten graph routes, not ten independently demonstrated end-to-end syntheses. Numerical conditions identified as proposed are experimental screening specifications, not claimed published protocols or guaranteed productive operating windows. External feedstocks include oxaloacetate, hydroxylamine, acetaldehyde and HCN; their formation is outside this network boundary. Neutral parent formulas represent acid-base pools except where charged species are required for redox accounting. Stereo-unspecified aminonitrile and amide nodes represent racemic precursor pools. The single environment_formed value records a representative formation environment where more than one applies.\n\nMOLECULES (21 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H+ (proton) [starting]\n  - mol_005: Fe (native iron) [starting]\n  - mol_006: Fe+2 (ferrous ion) [intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C3H4O3 (pyruvic acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C4H4O5 (oxaloacetic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_009: H3NO (hydroxylamine) [starting]\n  - mol_010: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_011: C3H7NO2 (L-alanine) [target]\n  - mol_012: C3H7NO2 (D-alanine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: FeH2O2 (ferrous hydroxide) [starting]\n  - mol_014: FeH3O3 (ferric hydroxide, formal hydrated Fe(III) sink) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: FeS (mackinawite) [starting]\n  - mol_016: H2S (hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_017: FeS2 (pyrite) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C2H4O (acetaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_019: CHN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_020: C3H6N2 (alaninonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_021: C3H8N2O (alaninamide) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (9 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_007, mol_003 (Awaruite-mediated hydrogen-dependent CO2 fixation to pyruvate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_002: mol_008 -> mol_007, mol_001 (Ferrous-ion-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_003: mol_008 -> mol_007, mol_001 (Uncatalyzed aqueous oxaloacetate decarboxylation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_007, mol_009, mol_005, mol_004 -> mol_011, mol_012, mol_006, mol_003 (Iron-driven hydroxylamine conversion of pyruvate to racemic alanine) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_005: mol_007, mol_010, mol_013, mol_003 -> mol_011, mol_012, mol_014 (Ferrous-hydroxide-driven reductive amination with ammonia) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_006: mol_007, mol_010, mol_015, mol_016 -> mol_011, mol_012, mol_017, mol_003 (Iron-sulfide and hydrogen-sulfide-supported reductive amination) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_007: mol_018, mol_019, mol_010 -> mol_020, mol_003 (Acetaldehyde Strecker formation of alaninonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_020, mol_003 -> mol_021 (Acid-catalyzed hydration of alaninonitrile to alaninamide) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_009: mol_021, mol_003 -> mol_011, mol_012, mol_010 (Alanine release by complete alaninamide hydrolysis) [Hydrothermal]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: CO2 fixation followed by hydroxylamine/iron amination (2 steps)\n  - P2: Iron-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by hydroxylamine amination (2 steps)\n  - P3: Surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by hydroxylamine amination (2 steps)\n  - P4: CO2 fixation followed by ferrous-hydroxide/ammonia amination (2 steps)\n  - P5: Iron-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by ferrous-hydroxide amination (2 steps)\n  - P6: Surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by ferrous-hydroxide amination (2 steps)\n  - P7: CO2 fixation followed by sulfide-associated ammonia amination (2 steps)\n  - P8: Iron-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by sulfide-associated amination (2 steps)\n  - P9: Surface oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by sulfide-associated amination (2 steps)\n  - P10: Acetaldehyde Strecker synthesis followed by complete staged hydrolysis (3 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 9 reactions | 4 balanced as written | 5 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_001 balanceable: 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> C3H4O3 + 3 H2O\n  - rxn_004 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 H3NO + 4 Fe + 8 H+ -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 4 Fe+2 + 4 H2O\n  - rxn_005 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 4 FeH2O2 + 2 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 4 FeH3O3\n  - rxn_006 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 FeS + 2 H2S -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 FeS2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_009 balanceable: 2 C3H8N2O + 2 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 NH3\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Alanine: Comprehensive Literature Review\n\n## 1. Known Prebiotic Synthesis Routes\n\nFour experimentally demonstrated pathways produce L-alanine (C3H7NO2, SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)) from prebiotically available precursors. These span hydrothermal, surface, and hybrid environments.\n\n### Pathway 1: Cyanosulfidic Nitrile Hydrolysis (Surface Environment)\n\nThe cyanosulfidic protometabolism pathway demonstrated by Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) shows that **2-aminopropanenitrile** (C3H6N2, SMILES: CC(C#N)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3) undergoes hydrolysis in aqueous conditions to yield L-alanine and ammonia.\n\n**Reaction**: 2-aminopropanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Alanine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of water to the nitrile group of 2-aminopropanenitrile, forming an amide intermediate, followed by further hydrolysis to the carboxylic acid (alanine) with release of ammonia (NH3, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3).\n\nThis is part of the broader Powell-Sutherland cyanosulfidic chemistry framework, in which HCN-derived nitriles serve as amino acid precursors via hydrolysis under wet-dry cycling or UV-irradiated surface pool conditions.\n\n### Pathway 2: Reductive Amination of Pyruvate with Hydroxylamine (Hydrothermal, Iron-Catalyzed)\n\nMuchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019, *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) demonstrated that **pyruvate** (C3H4O3, SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)) reacts with **hydroxylamine** (H3NO, SMILES: NO, InChI: InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2) in the presence of elemental iron (Fe\u2070) and ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a) to produce L-alanine.\n\nTwo variant conditions were reported:\n\n**Reaction A**: Pyruvate + hydroxylamine + H2O \u2192 L-Alanine (with water as co-substrate)\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO.O>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070, [Fe]) and **ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a, [Fe2+])\n- Mechanism: Iron-promoted reductive amination \u2014 hydroxylamine acts as the nitrogen donor, and iron species provide the reducing equivalents to drive the transamination. The \u03b1-keto acid (pyruvate) is converted to the corresponding \u03b1-amino acid (alanine) at mild hydrothermal temperatures.\n\n**Reaction B**: Pyruvate + hydroxylamine \u2192 L-Alanine + H2O (net dehydration)\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO>>CC(N)C(=O)O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070) and **ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n- Mechanism: Same iron-promoted reductive amination as Reaction A but with net water release. This represents the overall thermodynamic balance of the transformation.\n\nThese reactions are central to the Muchowska et al. demonstration that a simple iron-driven reaction network can interconvert universal metabolic precursors (\u03b1-keto acids \u2194 \u03b1-amino acids) under mild aqueous conditions with metallic iron.\n\n### Pathway 3: Hydrothermal Reduction of L-Serine (Hydrothermal)\n\nAubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2) showed that **L-serine** (C3H7NO3, SMILES: C(C(C(=O)O)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)) can be reduced to L-alanine by molecular hydrogen under submarine hydrothermal conditions.\n\n**Reaction**: L-Serine + H2 \u2192 L-Alanine\n- SMILES: N[C@H](CO)C(=O)O.[HH]>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Conditions: Elevated temperature and pressure characteristic of submarine hydrothermal vents\n- Mechanism: Reductive dehydroxylation \u2014 the hydroxymethyl group (-CH2OH) of serine is reduced to a methyl group (-CH3), converting serine to alanine. Molecular hydrogen (H2, SMILES: [HH]) serves as the reductant, which is abundantly available in hydrothermal vent fluids from serpentinization.\n\nThis pathway links alanine synthesis to the broader amino acid inventory of hydrothermal systems, where serine (itself formed via other prebiotic routes) undergoes reductive transformation.\n\n### Pathway 4: CO2 Fixation to Pyruvate via Fischer-Tropsch-Type Chemistry (Hydrothermal)\n\nPreiner, Igarashi, Muchowska, Yu, Varma, Kleinermanns, Nobu, Kamagata, T\u00fcys\u00fcz, Moran, and Martin (2020, *Nature Ecology & Evolution*, DOI: 10.1038/s41559-020-1125-6) demonstrated that **CO2** and **H2** can be converted directly to pyruvate (and water) on native iron and nickel-iron minerals at 100\u00b0C, providing an abiotic route to this critical metabolic hub molecule.\n\n**Reaction**: 3 CO2 + 5 H2 \u2192 Pyruvate + 3 H2O\n- SMILES: [HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].O=C=O.O=C=O.O=C=O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 100\u00b0C (373 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070, [Fe]), **awaruite** (Ni3Fe), and **magnetite** (Fe3O4, [O-2].[O-2].[O-2].[O-2].[Fe+2].[Fe+3].[Fe+3])\n- Conditions reported under three catalyst combinations:\n  - (a) Elemental Iron + awaruite at 100\u00b0C\n  - (b) Elemental Iron + magnetite at 100\u00b0C\n  - (c) Magnetite alone at 100\u00b0C\n- Mechanism: H2-dependent CO2 reduction on native metal surfaces \u2014 analogous to Fischer-Tropsch-type synthesis. Iron and nickel-iron alloy (awaruite) surfaces activate H2 and reduce CO2 stepwise through C1 intermediates (formate, formaldehyde, CO) to C2 (acetate) and then C3 (pyruvate) products. Magnetite provides an oxide surface that facilitates CO2 adsorption and reduction.\n\nThis is a key reaction because it connects the simplest geochemical feedstocks (CO2 and H2, both abundant in hydrothermal vent environments from serpentinization) to pyruvate, the central metabolic hub for alanine synthesis.\n\n## 2. Key Intermediates and Precursors\n\n### Hub Molecule: Pyruvate (C3H4O3)\n- Formula: C3H4O3\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\n- MW: 88.06\n- Role: **Central metabolic hub** \u2014 pyruvate is the key convergence point in the network. It is produced by at least four different upstream reactions (CO2 fixation, oxaloacetate decarboxylation, lactic acid oxidation, cyanosulfidic decarboxylation) and consumed by the reductive amination reactions that produce alanine. Pyruvate is a universal metabolic precursor in extant biology and its prebiotic availability is critical.\n\n### Upstream Precursors to Pyruvate\n\n**Oxaloacetate** (C4H4O5)\n- Formula: C4H4O5\n- SMILES: C(C(=O)C(=O)O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)\n- MW: 132.07\n- Role: Intermediate \u2014 a C4 dicarboxylic \u03b1-keto acid that undergoes decarboxylation to pyruvate\n\n**Lactic acid** (C3H6O3)\n- Formula: C3H6O3\n- SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)\n- MW: 90.08\n- Role: Intermediate \u2014 oxidized to pyruvate under cyanosulfidic conditions\n\n### Other Key Molecules\n\n**Hydroxylamine** (H3NO)\n- Formula: H3NO\n- SMILES: NO\n- InChI: InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\n- MW: 33.03\n- Role: Nitrogen donor for reductive amination of pyruvate to alanine\n\n**2-Aminopropanenitrile** (C3H6N2)\n- Formula: C3H6N2\n- SMILES: CC(C#N)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3\n- MW: 70.09\n- Role: Aminonitrile intermediate \u2014 Strecker-type precursor that hydrolyzes directly to alanine\n\n**L-Serine** (C3H7NO3)\n- Formula: C3H7NO3\n- SMILES: C(C(C(=O)O)N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)\n- MW: 105.09\n- Role: Amino acid precursor \u2014 reduced to alanine under hydrothermal conditions\n\n### Starting Molecules\n- **H2O**: SMILES: O, InChI: InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\n- **CO2**: SMILES: C(=O)=O, InChI: InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\n- **H2** (molecular hydrogen): SMILES: [HH], InChI: InChI=1S/H2/h1H\n- **NH3** (ammonia): SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\n\n## 3. Reaction Mechanisms and Conditions\n\n### Reactions Producing Pyruvate (Upstream)\n\n**Reaction U1: Oxaloacetate decarboxylation (iron-catalyzed)**\n- Oxaloacetate \u2192 Pyruvate + CO2\n- SMILES: O=C(O)CC(=O)C(=O)O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].O=C=O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: Ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n- Source: Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n- Mechanism: \u03b2-decarboxylation of oxaloacetate catalyzed by Fe\u00b2\u207a ions. This is thermodynamically favorable and proceeds readily at mild temperatures. Iron(II) acts as a Lewis acid to coordinate the \u03b2-keto acid and facilitate CO2 loss.\n\n**Reaction U2: Oxaloacetate decarboxylation (cyanosulfidic)**\n- Oxaloacetate \u2192 Pyruvate\n- SMILES: C(C(=O)C(=O)[O-])C(=O)[O-]>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-]\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Time: 1 hour\n- Source: Ritson (2021), *Science Advances*, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.abh3981\n- Mechanism: Decarboxylation under cyanosulfidic conditions as part of a prebiotic analog of the Krebs cycle.\n\n**Reaction U3: Lactic acid oxidation (cyanosulfidic)**\n- Lactic acid \u2192 Pyruvate\n- SMILES: CC(C(=O)[O-])O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-]\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Ritson (2021), *Science Advances*, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.abh3981\n- Mechanism: Oxidation of the \u03b1-hydroxy group of lactic acid to a ketone, converting lactate to pyruvate. This occurs under cyanosulfidic conditions where sulfur species act as oxidants.\n\n**Reaction U4: CO2 + H2 fixation to pyruvate (Fischer-Tropsch-type)**\n- 3 CO2 + 5 H2 \u2192 Pyruvate + 3 H2O\n- SMILES: [HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].[HH].O=C=O.O=C=O.O=C=O>>CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 100\u00b0C (373 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: Elemental Iron (Fe\u2070), awaruite (Ni3Fe), magnetite (Fe3O4)\n- Source: Preiner, Igarashi, Muchowska, Yu, Varma, Kleinermanns, Nobu, Kamagata, T\u00fcys\u00fcz, Moran, and Martin (2020), *Nature Ecology & Evolution*, DOI: 10.1038/s41559-020-1125-6\n\n### Reactions Producing L-Alanine (Final Steps)\n\n**Reaction F1: Nitrile hydrolysis**\n- 2-aminopropanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Alanine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n\n**Reaction F2: Reductive amination (variant A)**\n- Pyruvate + hydroxylamine + H2O \u2192 L-Alanine\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO.O>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal, 70\u00b0C, Fe\u2070 + Fe\u00b2\u207a\n- Source: Muchowska et al. (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n\n**Reaction F3: Reductive amination (variant B)**\n- Pyruvate + hydroxylamine \u2192 L-Alanine + 2 H2O\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].NO>>CC(N)C(=O)O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal, 70\u00b0C, Fe\u2070 + Fe\u00b2\u207a\n- Source: Muchowska et al. (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n\n**Reaction F4: Serine reduction**\n- L-Serine + H2 \u2192 L-Alanine\n- SMILES: N[C@H](CO)C(=O)O.[HH]>>C[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Source: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n\n## 4. Mineral Catalysts and Surfaces\n\nThe following mineral catalysts and metal species are experimentally documented for L-alanine synthesis reactions:\n\n- **Elemental Iron (Fe\u2070)**: Highly reactive, insoluble metallic iron. Acts as a strong reductant for CO2 fixation and drives reductive amination of pyruvate. Used in: pyruvate-to-alanine reductive amination (Muchowska et al., 2019) and CO2-to-pyruvate fixation (Preiner et al., 2020).\n\n- **Ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a)**: Soluble iron(II) species, abundant in anoxic early Earth oceans. Converts to insoluble Fe\u00b3\u207a in the presence of oxygen (not relevant on anoxic early Earth). Acts as Lewis acid catalyst for decarboxylation reactions and co-catalyst for reductive amination. Used in: oxaloacetate decarboxylation (Muchowska et al., 2019) and pyruvate-to-alanine reductive amination (Muchowska et al., 2019).\n\n- **Awaruite (Ni3Fe)**: A nickel-iron alloy mineral found in serpentinized ultramafic rocks. Provides catalytic surface for H2 activation and CO2 reduction. Used in: CO2-to-pyruvate Fischer-Tropsch-type synthesis (Preiner et al., 2020).\n\n- **Magnetite (Fe3O4)**: A mixed-valence iron oxide mineral common in hydrothermal systems. Facilitates CO2 adsorption and stepwise reduction. Used in: CO2-to-pyruvate synthesis (Preiner et al., 2020).\n\n## 5. Experimental Evidence\n\n### Study 1: Patel et al. (2015)\n- **Title**: \"Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Chemistry\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a unified cyanosulfidic chemistry starting from HCN and H2S can produce precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids. For alanine specifically, 2-aminopropanenitrile is formed via cyanosulfidic Strecker-like chemistry and hydrolyzes to alanine in aqueous conditions.\n\n### Study 2: Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019)\n- **Title**: \"Synthesis and breakdown of universal metabolic precursors promoted by iron\"\n- **Journal**: Nature\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that metallic iron (Fe\u2070) and ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a) at 70\u00b0C in water promote an interconnected network of reactions analogous to the core of biological metabolism. This includes: (a) interconversion of \u03b1-keto acids and \u03b1-amino acids via reductive amination with hydroxylamine, specifically pyruvate \u2192 alanine; (b) decarboxylation of oxaloacetate to pyruvate. This establishes a simple geochemical analog of the reductive TCA cycle and amino acid biosynthesis.\n\n### Study 3: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008)\n- **Title**: \"The Role of Submarine Hydrothermal Systems in the Synthesis of Amino Acids\"\n- **Journal**: Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres\n- **DOI**: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n- **Key finding**: Investigated amino acid synthesis under simulated submarine hydrothermal vent conditions. Demonstrated that L-serine can be reduced to L-alanine by molecular hydrogen, showing that hydrothermal processing can interconvert amino acids and expand the prebiotic amino acid inventory.\n\n### Study 4: Preiner et al. (2020)\n- **Title**: \"A hydrogen-dependent geochemical analogue of primordial carbon and energy metabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Ecology & Evolution\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/s41559-020-1125-6\n- **Key finding**: Showed that CO2 and H2 \u2014 the two simplest geochemical feedstocks available at serpentinizing hydrothermal vents \u2014 react on native iron, awaruite (Ni3Fe), and magnetite (Fe3O4) at 100\u00b0C to produce pyruvate and other metabolic intermediates. This provides a purely geochemical route from CO2 to the central metabolic hub (pyruvate) from which amino acids including alanine can be formed.\n\n### Study 5: Ritson (2021)\n- **Title**: \"A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle\"\n- **Journal**: Science Advances\n- **DOI**: 10.1126/sciadv.abh3981\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a cyanosulfidic reaction network can produce the intermediates of the Krebs (TCA) cycle abiotically. Relevant to alanine synthesis: (a) oxaloacetate undergoes decarboxylation to pyruvate under cyanosulfidic conditions; (b) lactic acid is oxidized to pyruvate. Both reactions provide additional prebiotic routes to the pyruvate hub molecule.\n\n## 6. Environmental Context\n\n### Hydrothermal Environment (Primary)\nThe majority of experimentally demonstrated alanine synthesis routes operate under hydrothermal conditions:\n- **Iron-catalyzed reductive amination** (Muchowska et al., 2019): Pyruvate + hydroxylamine \u2192 alanine at 70\u00b0C with Fe\u2070/Fe\u00b2\u207a. This mimics conditions at alkaline hydrothermal vents where metallic iron and dissolved Fe\u00b2\u207a are available from serpentinization, and temperatures of 70\u00b0C are found in the mixing zones between hot vent fluid and cooler ocean water.\n- **CO2 fixation to pyruvate** (Preiner et al., 2020): CO2 + H2 \u2192 pyruvate at 100\u00b0C on iron/awaruite/magnetite. Serpentinization of ultramafic rock produces abundant H2 and exposes native iron and nickel-iron alloys, making these minerals and conditions naturally co-located.\n- **Serine reduction** (Aubrey et al., 2008): L-serine + H2 \u2192 alanine under submarine hydrothermal conditions, where H2 from serpentinization provides the reductant.\n- **Oxaloacetate decarboxylation** (Muchowska et al., 2019): Fe\u00b2\u207a-catalyzed at 70\u00b0C.\n\n### Surface Environment (Secondary)\n- **Cyanosulfidic nitrile hydrolysis** (Patel et al., 2015): 2-aminopropanenitrile + H2O \u2192 alanine. This operates in surface pools where HCN-derived chemistry (UV-driven, evaporitic) produces aminonitrile intermediates that hydrolyze in wet phases.\n- **Cyanosulfidic Krebs cycle analogs** (Ritson, 2021): Lactic acid \u2192 pyruvate and oxaloacetate \u2192 pyruvate under surface cyanosulfidic conditions, feeding the pyruvate pool that is then available for transamination in environments where iron is present.\n\n### Environmental Interconnection\nThe synthesis network exhibits clear cross-environment flow:\n1. **Hydrothermal \u2192 Surface**: Pyruvate and other metabolic intermediates generated at hydrothermal vents could be transported to surface environments via ocean circulation.\n2. **Surface \u2192 Hydrothermal**: Cyanosulfidic products (aminonitriles, lactic acid, oxaloacetate) generated in surface pools could be washed into hydrothermal zones.\n3. **Both \u2192 Biochemical**: Alanine produced by any pathway enters the prebiotic amino acid pool available for peptide bond formation and proto-metabolic assembly.\n\n## 7. Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **Chirality**: All demonstrated prebiotic routes produce racemic alanine (equal L and D). The origin of homochirality (biological preference for L-amino acids) remains unresolved. Mineral surface adsorption or photolysis with circularly polarized light have been proposed.\n\n2. **Hydroxylamine availability**: The Muchowska et al. pathway requires hydroxylamine (NH2OH), whose prebiotic abundance is debated. It could form from NO reduction by Fe\u2070 or from NH3 oxidation, but quantitative constraints are needed.\n\n3. **Concentration problem**: Hydrothermal vent products are diluted in the ocean. Mechanisms for concentrating pyruvate and other intermediates (evaporitic pools, mineral adsorption, thermophoresis) are important for downstream reactions.\n\n4. **2-Aminopropanenitrile stability**: The Patel et al. pathway requires this intermediate to persist long enough for hydrolysis. Its stability under UV irradiation and varying pH conditions needs further study.\n\n5. **Pyruvate stability**: Pyruvate is thermally labile and can decarboxylate to acetaldehyde + CO2 at elevated temperatures. The balance between pyruvate production and decomposition at hydrothermal temperatures is a key kinetic question.\n\n=== END CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH ===\n\nConsider how well the network aligns with this literature in your evaluation.\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\",\n            \"approved\": <true if confidence >= 0.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote: \"environmental_progression\" scores Environmental Consistency (conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.94\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.9,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.9,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.75,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.94,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.86,\n      \"feedback\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) and Muchowska et al. (2019) provide relevant experimental support for the constituent transformations. Both reactions conserve atoms and charge with the supplied coefficients. The hydroxylamine step correctly requires four reducing electrons per alanine. The principal uncertainty is whether the fixation stage delivers usable pyruvate concentrations under conditions compatible with subsequent iron-mediated amination. Racemic production satisfies the stated L-alanine-presence claim.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify awaruite preparation and loading, gas pressures, pH, temperature and residence time, distinguishing the Preiner protocol from proposed screening conditions.\",\n        \"Define the interstage handling and measure pyruvate survival, concentration and alanine yield in the actual fixation-product mixture.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.94\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.95,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.95,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.82,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.96,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.89,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is the best-supported route within the declared feedstock boundary. Ferrous-ion-promoted oxaloacetate decarboxylation and hydroxylamine/iron conversion of pyruvate to alanine align with Muchowska et al. (2019). The intermediates and reducing-equivalent accounting are appropriate. Compatible mild hydrothermal operation is plausible, although the network does not supply a complete sequential protocol. No intrinsically invalid reaction is identified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the initial dissolved Fe2+ inventory, Fe0 loading, pH, temperature, reagent concentrations and addition sequence.\"